predicts \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\) for the original tax and is contradicted directly.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind correction --text 'Verifier #28 is incorrect for Q Eq. (40), lines 412\u2013422: it omits the third term\u2019s factor \\\\\\\\(N/(N-1)\\\\\\\\). At common price \\\\\\\\(p_m=p\\\\\\\\), write \\\\\\\\(z=\\\\\\\\sum_mx_m-c\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(d_n=x_n-c/N\\\\\\\\). The price-quadratic terms cancel, the third term is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha Np\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top d_n/(N-1)\\\\\\\\), and the final term is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\alpha[p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top d_n-p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top z]/(N-1)\\\"'^2'\\\"\\\\\\\\). Thus \\\\\\\\(t_n/\\\\\\\\alpha=[N/(N-1)+1/(N-1)\\\"'^2]p''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top d_n-[1/(N-1)+1/(N-1)\\\"'^2]p''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top z\\\\\\\\). At complementary equilibrium \\\\\\\\(p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top z=0\\\\\\\\), the coefficient is \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\kappa_N=(N\\\"'^2-N+1)/(N-1)''^2'\\\"\\\\\\\\), as in #12\u201313 and ada/pair_note.md. The rebate in #13 contributes \\\\\\\\(-\\\\\\\\alpha Np\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top d_n/(N-1)\\\"'^2'\\\"\\\\\\\\), leaving \\\\\\\\(t_n\\\"'^{'\\\"\\\\\\\\rm new}=\\\\\\\\alpha p\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\top d_n\\\\\\\\), so balance and IR remain valid. For \\\\\\\\(N=3,c=3,x=(2,1,0),p=1,n=1,\\\\\\\\alpha=1\\\\\\\\), Eq. (40) gives \\\\\\\\(t_1=7/4\\\\\\\\), rebate \\\\\\\\(-3/4\\\\\\\\), and revised transfer \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\); #28 predicts \\\\\\\\(1\\\\\\\\) for the original tax and is contradicted directly.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"29\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The direct substitution supports the existing coefficient: at \\\\(N=3\\\\), Q\u2019s tax is \\\\(7/4\\\\) in a concrete complementary profile, while the verifier\u2019s formula gives \\\\(1\\\\). I\u2019ve recorded the correction in the ledger and am documenting the full expansion.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: style-gates\\ndescription: >-\\n  Run and enforce lightweight style gates for Markdown, LaTeX, and PDF artifacts before Codex\\n  shares, commits, pushes, or produces .md, .tex, or .pdf files. Use when writing, editing,\\n  reviewing, generating, or pushing reports, papers, slides, documentation, markdown notes,\\n  TeX sources, or PDFs that should pass basic style and integrity checks.\\n---\\n\\n# Style Gates\\n\\n## Core Rule\\n\\nBefore finalizing, sharing, committing, or pushing any `.md`, `.tex`, or `.pdf` artifact,\\nidentify the affected files and run the gate. Fix failures before delivery. If a failure is\\nintentionally left unresolved, say exactly which gate failed and why.\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nFrom the repository root, run:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed\\n```\\n\\nBefore commit or push, prefer:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --staged\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed --base origin/main\\n```\\n\\nFor a specific artifact:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py report.md paper.tex slides.pdf\\n```\\n\\nUse `--strict-warnings` when the recipient wants warnings to block delivery.\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n1. Determine scope:\\n   - For generated files, pass the exact output paths.\\n   - For a commit, use `--staged`.\\n   - For a branch or push, use `--changed --base origin/main` or the relevant upstream base.\\n   - For an initial audit, use `--all`.\\n2. Run the gate script.\\n3. Fix all errors. Treat warnings as review prompts unless `--strict-warnings` is required.\\n4. For LaTeX and PDFs, also use project-native validation when available:\\n   - Compile `.tex` with the repo's existing command or `latexmk`/`pdflatex`.\\n   - Open or inspect generated PDFs enough to confirm they are not blank, corrupt, or stale.\\n5. Re-run the gate after edits.\\n\\n## Human Review Gates\\n\\nThe script catches mechanical issues; still review these before sharing.\\n\\nMarkdown:\\n- Clear title and section hierarchy.\\n- No unresolved placeholders, merge markers, stale TODOs, or fake links.\\n- Tables, lists, math, and fenced code render plausibly.\\n- Claims with external facts have sources or are clearly marked as assumptions.\\n\\nLaTeX:\\n- The file compiles, or the reason it was not compiled is reported.\\n- No unresolved citations/references in the build log when a PDF is produced.\\n- No obvious placeholder text, broken environments, or empty `\\\\ref{}` / `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n- Generated PDFs correspond to the current `.tex`, not an older build artifact.\\n\\nPDF:\\n- File opens, has at least one page, and is not zero-byte/corrupt.\\n- Expected text is visible/extractable unless the PDF is intentionally image-only.\\n- No accidental blank pages, cropped content, or stale draft/watermark text.\\n\\n## Integration\\n\\nFor repo hooks, CI, and copy/paste setup, read `references/repo-integration.md`.\\nTemplates live in `assets/`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom fractions import Fraction as F\\nfor n in range(2, 8):\\n    c=F(3); x=[F(2),F(1)]+[F(0)]*(n-2); p=[F(1)]*n; k=0; a=F(1)\\n    bp=sum(p[:k]+p[k+1:]); bx=sum(x[:k]+x[k+1:]); bpp=sum(q*q for i,q in enumerate(p) if i\\\"'!=k); bpx=sum(p[i]*x[i] for i in range(n) if i!=k)\\n    tax=a*(F(1,2)*p[k]*(p[k]-F(2,n-1)*bp)-F(1,n-1)*p[k]*(x[k]+bx-c)+F(n,(n-1)**2)*bp*(x[k]-c/n)+F(1,2*(n-1))*bpp-F(1,(n-1)**2)*(bpx-bp*c/n))\\n    rebate=a*F(n,(n-1)**3)*bp*(bx-F(n-1,n)*c)\\n    kappa=F(n*n-n+1,(n-1)**2)\\n    assert tax == kappa*(x[k]-c/n)\\n    assert tax+rebate == x[k]-c/n\\n    print(n, '\\\"'tax=',tax,'rebate=',rebate,'revised=',tax+rebate)\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom fractions import Fraction as F\\nfor n in range(2, 8):\\n    c=F(3); x=[F(2),F(1)]+[F(0)]*(n-2); p=[F(1)]*n; k=0; a=F(1)\\n    bp=sum(p[:k]+p[k+1:]); bx=sum(x[:k]+x[k+1:]); bpp=sum(q*q for i,q in enumerate(p) if i\\\"'!=k); bpx=sum(p[i]*x[i] for i in range(n) if i!=k)\\n    tax=a*(F(1,2)*p[k]*(p[k]-F(2,n-1)*bp)-F(1,n-1)*p[k]*(x[k]+bx-c)+F(n,(n-1)**2)*bp*(x[k]-c/n)+F(1,2*(n-1))*bpp-F(1,(n-1)**2)*(bpx-bp*c/n))\\n    rebate=a*F(n,(n-1)**3)*bp*(bx-F(n-1,n)*c)\\n    kappa=F(n*n-n+1,(n-1)**2)\\n    assert tax == kappa*(x[k]-c/n)\\n    assert tax+rebate == x[k]-c/n\\n    print(n, '\\\"'tax=',tax,'rebate=',rebate,'revised=',tax+rebate)\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"2 tax= 3/2 rebate= -1 revised= 1/2\\n3 tax= 7/4 rebate= -3/4 revised= 1\\n4 tax= 65/36 rebate= -5/9 revised= 5/4\\n5 tax= 147/80 rebate= -7/16 revised= 7/5\\n6 tax= 93/50 rebate= -9/25 revised= 3/2\\n7 tax= 473/252 rebate= -11/36 revised= 11/7\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I\u2019m using the style-gates skill for the derivation note. An exact-rational check for \\\\(N=2,\\\\ldots,7\\\\) matches the expansion and the revised transfer.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/emmy/transfer_audit.md\",\"kind\":\"add\"}],\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/emmy/transfer_audit.md\",\"kind\":\"add\"}],\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' emmy/transfer_audit.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' emmy/transfer_audit.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# General-agent transfer audit\\n\\nQ's proposed payment is Eq. (40), [Q, lines 412\u2013422](../inputs/Q.tex).\\nThe bars there are **sums** over the other agents. This distinction fixes\\nthe coefficient disputed in ledger entry 28.\\n\\nLet \\\\(S=\\\\sum_m x_m\\\\), \\\\(z=S-c\\\\), and \\\\(d_n=x_n-c/N\\\\). At a common price\\n\\\\(p_m=p\\\\) for every agent, Q's definitions give\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\bar p_{-n}=(N-1)p,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bar\\\\sigma^p_{-n}=(N-1)p^\\\\top p,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\bar\\\\sigma^{px}_{-n}=p^\\\\top(S-x_n).\\n\\\\]\\nThe first and fourth terms of Eq. (40) cancel. Its second, third, and\\nfifth terms, after division by \\\\(\\\\alpha\\\\), are respectively\\n\\\\[\\n-\\\\frac{p^\\\\top z}{N-1},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{N}{N-1}p^\\\\top d_n,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\frac{p^\\\\top d_n-p^\\\\top z}{(N-1)^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the exact common-price identity, before invoking complementarity, is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{t_n}{\\\\alpha}\\n=\\\\left(\\\\frac{N}{N-1}+\\\\frac{1}{(N-1)^2}\\\\right)p^\\\\top d_n\\n-\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{N-1}+\\\\frac{1}{(N-1)^2}\\\\right)p^\\\\top z.\\n\\\\]\\nAt an equilibrium, Q's price best-response conditions imply \\\\(p\\\\ge0\\\\),\\n\\\\(z\\\\le0\\\\), and \\\\(p^\\\\top z=0\\\\). Therefore\\n\\\\[\\nt_n=\\\\alpha\\\\kappa_Np^\\\\top d_n,\\\\qquad\\n\\\\kappa_N=\\\\frac{N^2-N+1}{(N-1)^2}=1+\\\\frac{N}{(N-1)^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe verifier's coefficient \\\\((N+1)/(N-1)^2\\\\) omits the factor\\n\\\\(N/(N-1)\\\\) from Q's third term. For a direct check, take\\n\\\\(N=3\\\\), \\\\(c=3\\\\), \\\\(x=(2,1,0)\\\\), \\\\(p_m=1\\\\), \\\\(n=1\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\).\\nThe five terms of Eq. (40) are \\\\(-1/2\\\\), \\\\(0\\\\), \\\\(3/2\\\\), \\\\(1/2\\\\), and\\n\\\\(1/4\\\\). Hence \\\\(t_1=7/4\\\\), whereas the verifier's formula yields\\n\\\\(1\\\\). These messages have common prices and complementarity; the\\ncalculation of Eq. (40) does not require choosing valuations.\\n\\nThe proposed others-only rebate is\\n\\\\[\\ng_n(s_{-n})=\\n\\\\frac{\\\\alpha N}{(N-1)^3}\\\\bar p_{-n}^\\\\top\\n\\\\left(\\\\bar x_{-n}-\\\\frac{N-1}{N}c\\\\right).\\n\\\\]\\nAt a common-price complementary equilibrium, its value is\\n\\\\(g_n=-\\\\alpha Np^\\\\top d_n/(N-1)^2\\\\), so\\n\\\\(t_n+g_n=\\\\alpha p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\). In the \\\\(N=3\\\\) check, the rebate\\nis \\\\(-3/4\\\\), and the revised payment is \\\\(1\\\\).\\n\\nSince \\\\(g_n\\\\) uses only other agents' messages, it leaves every best\\nresponse unchanged. The revised payments sum to\\n\\\\(\\\\alpha p^\\\\top(S-c)=0\\\\). If \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\),\\n\\\\(V_n(0)=0\\\\), and equilibrium demand maximizes\\n\\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\nV_n(x_n)-(t_n+g_n)\\n=V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n+\\\\alpha p^\\\\top c/N\\n\\\\ge \\\\alpha p^\\\\top c/N\\\\ge0,\\n\\\\]\\nwhere the last inequality also uses \\\\(c\\\\ge0\\\\). Thus the transfer\\ncalculation still supports equilibrium budget balance and individual\\nrationality. The Q\u2013P finite-sample welfare and regret bounds in\\n[Ada's pair note](../ada/pair_note.md) remain conditional on the\\nexistence of a surrogate equilibrium and a planner multiplier. The\\nsampling result in [P, lines 1287\u20131315](../inputs/P.tex) does not prove\\nlearning convergence for this mechanism.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/.local/bin/style-ban-artifacts emmy/transfer_audit.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/.local/bin/style-ban-artifacts emmy/transfer_audit.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'python3 /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py emmy/transfer_audit.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'python3 /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py emmy/transfer_audit.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"style-gate: checked 1 file(s), 0 error(s), 0 warning(s)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba inputs/Q.tex | sed -n '115,145p;150,180p;200,230p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba inputs/Q.tex | sed -n '115,145p;150,180p;200,230p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   115\\t\\\\par\\n   116\\tThe network manager aims to maximize total social welfare by solving\\n   117\\t\\\\begin{subequations}\\\\label{manager_problem_eq}\\n   118\\t\\t\\\\begin{align}\\n   119\\t\\t  &\\\\max_{x\\\\in \\\\mathcal{X}}{\\\\sum_{n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{V_n(x_n)}}\\\\\\\\ \\n   120\\t\\t  \\\\text{s.t.}\\\\quad & \\\\sum_{n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{x_n}\\\\leq c,\\n   121\\t\\t\\\\end{align}\\n   122\\t\\\\end{subequations}\\n   123\\twhere $x:=\\\\textbf{col}(x_1,\\\\dots,x_N)$, $\\\\mathcal{X}:=\\\\Pi_{n\\\\in \\\\mathcal{N}}\\\\mathcal{X}_n$, and $c\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}_{\\\\geq 0}^K$ denotes the amount of available resources.\\n   124\\t\\\\begin{assumption}\\\\label{agent_set_assumption}\\n   125\\tFor each agent $n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}$, the strategy set $\\\\mathcal{X}_n$ is convex and compact. \\n   126\\t\\\\end{assumption}\\n   127\\t\\\\begin{assumption}\\\\label{valuation_function_assumption}\\n   128\\tFor each agent $n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}$, the valuation function $V_n(x_n):\\\\mathcal{X}_n\\\\rightarrow \\\\mathbb{R}$ is continuous, twice differentiable, $\\\\alpha$-strongly concave, i.e., $\\\\forall x_n\\\\in \\\\mathcal{X}_n:$\\n   129\\t\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\label{alpha_concave_eq}\\n   130\\t\\\\begin{split}\\n   131\\t    &(\\\\nabla V_n(x_n)-\\\\nabla V_n(x_n'))^\\\\top(x_n-x_n')\\\\leq-\\\\alpha\\\\|x_n-x_n'\\\\|^2,\\n   132\\t\\\\end{split}\\n   133\\t\\\\end{equation*}\\n   134\\tand $V_n(0)=0$.\\n   135\\t\\\\end{assumption}\\n   136\\t\\\\begin{remark}\\n   137\\tThe imposition of conditions in Assumption \\\\ref{valuation_function_assumption} on the valuation functions $V_n(x_n)$ is less strict compared to assuming the conditions for all samples of the stochastic function $\\\\psi_n(x_n;\\\\xi_n)$.\\n   138\\t\\\\end{remark}\\n   139\\tThe resource allocation problem \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq} with network coupling constraints cannot be solved in a centralized fashion due to the network manager's lack of knowledge about the agents' valuation functions and local constraints. In addition, agents might opt for a different strategy from the optimal solution of optimization problem \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq} due to their selfishness and their desire to maximize their own utility. To address these challenges, we propose an incentive mechanism.\\n   140\\t%\\n   141\\t\\\\subsection{Incentive mechanism design problem}\\\\label{machanism_design_subsec}\\n   142\\t\\n   143\\t\\\\par\\n   144\\tThe network manager seeks to design an incentive mechanism that imposes a non-cooperative game among the agents, where the equilibrium of the game is the optimal solution to problem \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq}. To achieve this goal, the manager asks the agents to request resources and propose prices for them. Then, the manager imposes taxes or provides subsidies to the agents through payment functions, which depend on their requested resources and proposed prices, which steers the agents towards choosing the socially optimal strategies.\\n   145\\t\\\\par\\n   150\\t\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{utility_maximization_eq}\\n   151\\t\\t\\\\max_{s_n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}_n}{[U_n(s_n, s_{-n}):=}V_n(x_n)-t_n(s_n, s_{-n})].\\n   152\\t\\\\end{equation}\\n   153\\t\\\\par\\n   154\\tSince the payment function of each agent is affected by the strategies of other agents, a non-cooperative game is induced among the agents, which is denoted by $\\\\mathcal{G}:=(\\\\mathcal{N}, (\\\\mathcal{S}_n)_{n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}, U_n(s_n, s_{-n}))_{n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}$. \\n   155\\tThe Nash equilibrium (NE) of the induced game is defined as follows:\\n   156\\t\\n   157\\t\\\\begin{definition}[Nash Equilibrium]\\\\label{nash_def}\\n   158\\t\\tThe strategy vector $s^*=\\\\textbf{col}(s_1^*, \\\\ldots, s_N^*)$ is a Nash equilibrium of game $\\\\mathcal{G}$ if:\\n   159\\t\\t\\\\[\\n   160\\t\\t\\tU_n(s_n^*, s_{-n}^*)\\\\geq U_n(s_n, s_{-n}^*), \\\\quad \\\\forall n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}.\\n   161\\t\\t\\\\]\\n   162\\t\\\\end{definition}\\n   163\\tThis implies that, at a Nash equilibrium, no agent can strictly improve its utility by unilaterally deviating from their equilibrium strategy, given that all other agents keep their strategies fixed. \\n   164\\t\\n   165\\tThe manager aims to design the payment function $t_n$ in game $\\\\mathcal{G}$ such that the mechanism satisfies the following desired properties:\\n   166\\t\\\\begin{enumerate}[P1)]\\n   167\\t\\t\\\\item \\\\textbf{Existence and Uniqueness of NE:} There exists a unique equilibrium $s^*:=\\\\textbf{col}(s_1^*,\\\\ldots,s_N^*)$ for the induced game.\\n   168\\t\\t\\\\item\\n   169\\t\\t\\\\textbf{Nash implementation:} The  allocated resources at a NE of the induced game $\\\\mathcal{G}$ are equal to the optimal solution $x^o$ of problem \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq}, i.e., $x_n^*=x_n^o, \\\\, \\\\forall n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}$.\\n   170\\t\\t\\\\item\\n   171\\t\\t\\\\textbf{Budget balance:} At the NE of the game, the total payment of agents through the whole network is equal to zero, i.e., $\\\\sum_{n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{t_n(s_n^*, s_{-n}^*)}=0$.\\n   172\\t        \\\\item\\n   173\\t\\t\\\\textbf{Individual rationality:} All users participate in the mechanism voluntarily, in a sense that their utility at the NE of the game is at least equal to their utility when they do not participate in the mechanism, i.e., $U_n(s_n^*,s_{-n}^*)\\\\geq V_n(0)=0, \\\\, \\\\forall n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}$.\\n   174\\t\\t\\\\item\\n   175\\t\\t\\\\textbf{Dynamic stability:} The manager provides an iterative algorithm, along which the agents can learn their NE strategy.\\n   176\\t\\\\end{enumerate}\\n   177\\t\\n   178\\t\\\\par\\n   179\\tThe payment function $t_n$ is chosen from a class of parameterized quadratic functions as  follows:\\n   180\\t\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{tax_function_eq}\\n   200\\t\\\\begin{proposition}\\\\label{existence_uniqueness_prop}\\n   201\\tDefine the matrix $\\\\Psi=(\\\\Psi_{nm})_{n,m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}$ where \\n   202\\t\\\\[\\\\Psi_{nn}=-\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n   203\\t    \\\\alpha I_K & B_{nn}^n\\\\\\\\\\n   204\\t        B_{nn}^{n^\\\\top}& A_{nn}^n\\n   205\\t\\\\end{pmatrix}, \\n   206\\t\\\\Psi_{nm}=-\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n   207\\t    0 & B_{mn}^n\\\\\\\\\\n   208\\t    B_{nm}^{n^\\\\top} & A_{nm}^n\\n   209\\t\\\\end{pmatrix},  m\\\\neq n.\\\\] A Nash equilibrium of $\\\\mathcal{G}$ exists and is unique if\\n   210\\t\\t\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{existence_uniqueness_cond_eq}\\n   211\\t\\t\\\\textnormal{P1:} \\\\left\\\\{\\n   212\\t\\t(\\\\romannumeral 1) \\\\Psi_{nn}\\\\preceq 0, \\\\,\\n   213\\t        (\\\\romannumeral 2) \\\\Psi + \\\\Psi^\\\\top \\\\preceq -\\\\upsilon I_{2KN}\\n   214\\t\\t\\\\right\\\\},\\n   215\\t\\t\\\\end{equation}\\n   216\\t    where $\\\\upsilon>0$ is a scalar design parameter. \\n   217\\t\\\\end{proposition}\\n   218\\t\\\\begin{proof}\\n   219\\tDefine $J:=(J_{nm})_{n,m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}$ where\\n   220\\t\\\\[\\n   221\\tJ_{nm}:=\\\\frac{\\\\partial^2 U_n(s_n,s_{-n})}{\\\\partial s_n \\\\partial s_m}.\\n   222\\t\\\\]\\n   223\\tBy Assumption \\\\ref{valuation_function_assumption}, we have $\\\\frac{\\\\partial^2 V_n(s_n)}{\\\\partial s_n^2}\\\\preceq -\\\\alpha I_K$, thus\\n   224\\t\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{unique_eq}\\n   225\\t     J_{nn}=\\n   226\\t     \\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n   227\\t        \\\\frac{\\\\partial^2 V_n(s_n)}{\\\\partial s_n^2} & -B_{nn}^n\\\\\\\\\\n   228\\t        -B_{nn}^{n^\\\\top}& -A_{nn}^n\\n   229\\t    \\\\end{pmatrix}\\\\preceq \\n   230\\t    \\\\Psi_{nn}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/emmy/transfer_audit.md\",\"kind\":\"update\"}],\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q