), rebate \\\\(-3/4\\\\), revised tax \\\\(1\\\\). The coefficient in #28 instead predicts \\\\(t_1=1\\\\). Static Q\u2013P conclusions in #17/#22 survive this audit; implementable learning and novelty remain unresolved.\\nlatest substantive entry: 30\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy ready --seen 30'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy ready --seen 30'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"31\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_29\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I\u2019m ready at ledger entry #31. The strongest Q\u2013P connection is a **conditional static result**: use P\u2019s sampled CVaR estimate to form risk adjusted valuations, then use Q\u2019s allocation game with an others-only rebate. Under strong concavity, a planner multiplier, and feasible oracle queries, an equilibrium of the surrogate game has exact budget balance and individual rationality for the surrogate. P\u2019s error estimate then bounds its true welfare loss by \\\\(2\\\\sum_n h_n\\\\) and each agent\u2019s true unilateral gain by \\\\(2h_n\\\\), where \\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}/(\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\). The derivation uses [Q\u2019s tax](</Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/inputs/Q.tex:412>) and [P\u2019s assumptions and CVaR bound](</Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/inputs/P.tex:1287>).\\n\\nI checked the verifier\u2019s general-\\\\(N\\\\) objection directly. Its payment coefficient omits a term from Q\u2019s tax: the original equilibrium coefficient is \\\\((N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2\\\\). A genuine \\\\(N=3\\\\) equilibrium gives original payment \\\\(7/4\\\\), rebate \\\\(-3/4\\\\), and revised payment \\\\(1\\\\). The expansion and example are in [emmy/transfer_audit.md](</Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/emmy/transfer_audit.md>); both style checks pass.\\n\\nA publication would still need an implementable learning and convergence result for heterogeneous allocations. P\u2019s consensus algorithm addresses a shared decision, and this pair does not establish novelty.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"turn.completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":546153,\"cached_input_tokens\":511104,\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":8756,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":3554}}"], "error": null, "rates": {"input_per_m": 2.0, "cached_input_per_m": 0.2, "output_per_m": 10.0}}, {"v": 2, "at": "2026-09-25T17:48:56Z", "thread": "Q7P1", "stage": "peer", "actor": "ada", "backend": "codex", "model": "gpt-6-sol", "outcome": "completed", "seconds": 113.1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 391359, "cached_input_tokens": 359936, "cache_write_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 5082, "reasoning_output_tokens": 2171}, "input_tokens": 391359, "output_tokens": 5082, "cache_write": 0, "cache_read": 359936, "prefix_read": null, "cost": 0.18565320000000002, "cost_basis": "priced", "exit_status": 0, "terminal_event": "{\"type\":\"turn.completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":391359,\"cached_input_tokens\":359936,\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":5082,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":2171}}", "raw_events": ["{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a0d9ad-8abb-7363-af97-07015a628f99\"}", "{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I\u2019ll read the ledger first, then check Q and P against the current claims and add any supported finding or objection.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 0'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 0'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#1 [ada/intention] I am comparing Q\u2019s LMI-designed balanced incentive game and mean-square best-response dynamics with P\u2019s sampled CVaR zeroth-order optimization. I will first test whether P\u2019s risk objective can be implemented by Q\u2019s mechanism assumptions, then examine whether its sampling/communication method yields a genuinely new stability statement. I will cite exact paper lines and report obstructions.\\n#2 [emmy/intention] I am testing whether Q\u2019s quadratic tax and strong-monotonicity argument extend to risk-adjusted valuations \\\\(V_n(x_n)=-\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{\\\\beta_n}(J_n(x_n,\\\\xi_n))\\\\), with P supplying a black-box empirical CVaR oracle. I will check Q\u2019s exact curvature and sample-error requirements against P\u2019s assumptions, and look for a smaller theorem or counterexample if a direct algorithm transplant fails.\\n#3 [emmy/objection] A direct transplant of P\u2019s Algorithm 1 into Q is invalid: P (Preliminaries and Eq. (update y)) has every agent maintain a copy of one common decision \\\\(x\\\\in\\\\mathcal X\\\\), and proves consensus; Q (manager problem (1)) optimizes heterogeneous allocations \\\\((x_1,\\\\ldots,x_N)\\\\) under \\\\(\\\\sum_n x_n\\\\le c\\\\). Consensus can destroy welfare: for \\\\(V_1(x)=2x-x^2/2\\\\), \\\\(V_2(x)=x-x^2/2\\\\), \\\\(c=1\\\\), the optimum is \\\\((1,0)\\\\), while equal allocations \\\\((1/2,1/2)\\\\) lose \\\\(1/4\\\\). P also assumes \\\\(r\\\\mathbb B\\\\subseteq\\\\mathcal X\\\\), incompatible with Q\u2019s nonnegative allocation sets if copied literally. The reusable object is P\u2019s empirical-CVaR oracle/error bound, not its consensus iteration.\\n#4 [ada/idea] Sharpened question: can Q\u2019s quadratic tax implement a risk-aware allocation when only P\u2019s black-box sampled losses are available, with quantitative economic guarantees for the true CVaR game? Use P\u2019s expected empirical CVaR plus ball smoothing as a fixed convex surrogate, add \\\\(\\\\gamma\\\\|x_n\\\\|^2/2\\\\) to ensure Q\u2019s strong concavity, and normalize each valuation at \\\\(x_n=0\\\\). P\u2019s bounded-loss DKW lemma (Appendix, Lemma cvar-estimation error bound) gives uniform-in-point \\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}/(\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\) value error. Conditional on Q\u2019s tax/LMI certificate for the surrogate, its unique equilibrium has true planner loss at most \\\\(2\\\\sum_n h_n\\\\), true unilateral regret at most \\\\(4h_n\\\\), exact payment balance, and true individual utility at least \\\\(-2h_n\\\\). This is a candidate theorem, not yet checked for the oracle-domain and Q proof issues. Emmy\u2019s #3 correctly rules out importing P\u2019s consensus iteration; I intend to use only its local CVaR estimator.\\n#5 [ada/objection] Major correction to my #4: Q\u2019s advertised LMI certificate is algebraically infeasible. Q Theorem nash_implementation_theorem, P2(i) (inputs/Q.tex around 240\u2013250) requires \\\\(\\\\sum_m A_{nm}^n=0\\\\) for every \\\\(n\\\\). Q Proposition existence_uniqueness_prop, P1(ii) (around 200\u2013213) requires \\\\(\\\\Psi+\\\\Psi^\\\\top\\\\preceq-\\\\upsilon I\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\upsilon>0\\\\), and its price-price block in row \\\\(n,m\\\\) is \\\\(-A_{nm}^n\\\\). Set a nonzero direction \\\\(v\\\\) with every allocation component zero and every price component the same \\\\(h\\\\). Then \\\\(v^\\\\top\\\\Psi v=-\\\\sum_n h^\\\\top(\\\\sum_m A_{nm}^n)h=0\\\\), hence \\\\(v^\\\\top(\\\\Psi+\\\\Psi^\\\\top)v=0\\\\), contradicting strict negativity. Thus no tax can satisfy Q\u2019s stated P1 and P2 LMIs jointly, including Eq. (40); Q Proposition prop2 is false as stated. This does not by itself disprove equilibrium uniqueness via another argument. It blocks invoking Q\u2019s strong-monotonicity certificate for the proposed CVaR hybrid. I am checking whether a narrower value-stability result survives without that certificate.\\n#6 [emmy/objection] Critical check on Q: its proposed payment (Eq. (40)) cannot satisfy its own strict P1 LMI (Eq. (existence_uniqueness_cond_eq)), despite Proposition (prop2). For any \\\\(N\\\\ge2\\\\), its price-dependent own-price derivative is \\\\(\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-\\\\alpha[p_n-\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1)]+\\\\alpha(\\\\sum_m x_m-c)/(N-1)\\\\). Holding \\\\(x\\\\) fixed and moving all prices by the same vector \\\\(h\\\\) leaves this pseudogradient component unchanged. Thus the price-price Jacobian has common-price null vector \\\\((h,\\\\ldots,h)\\\\), so the full symmetric pseudogradient Jacobian has zero quadratic form on \\\\((\\\\Delta x=0,\\\\Delta p=(h,\\\\ldots,h))\\\\); it cannot be \\\\(\\\\preceq-\\\\upsilon I\\\\) for \\\\(\\\\upsilon>0\\\\). This blocks using Q\u2019s claimed explicit LMI certificate as a theorem. It does not by itself prove nonunique NE; a different uniqueness proof or redesigned tax is needed. P\u2019s CVaR oracle still provides a separate route to quantitative approximate-equilibrium guarantees.\\n#7 [ada/finding] Beyond the LMI contradiction in #5/#6, Eq. (40) can have genuinely nonunique Nash equilibria. Take \\\\(N=2,K=1,c=2,\\\\mathcal X_1=\\\\mathcal X_2=[0,1],\\\\alpha=1\\\\), and \\\\(V_n(x)=2x-x^2/2\\\\). For each \\\\(p\\\\in[0,1]\\\\), the profile \\\\((x_1,p_1,x_2,p_2)=(1,p,1,p)\\\\) is NE. Directly from Q Eq. (40), \\\\(\\\\partial_{x_n}U_n=V_n\\\\prime(x_n)+p_n-2p_{-n}\\\\), \\\\(\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-p_n+p_{-n}+x_1+x_2-c\\\\), and the own-strategy Hessian is \\\\(\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-1&1\\\\\\\\1&-1\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\preceq0\\\\). At the profile, these derivatives are \\\\(1-p\\\\ge0\\\\) at upper bound \\\\(x_n=1\\\\), and \\\\(0\\\\) for price, proving a global best response. Thus Q\u2019s claimed unique NE fails under its stated compact, strongly concave valuation assumptions; optimum allocation remains unique. This is compatible with P\u2019s genuinely stochastic risk model: let \\\\(\\\\xi=\\\\pm 1/4\\\\) equiprobably, \\\\(J_n(x,\\\\xi)=x^2/2-(9/4+\\\\xi)x\\\\), and take tail level \\\\(\\\\beta=1/2\\\\); then \\\\(\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{1/2}J_n(x,\\\\xi)=x^2/2-2x\\\\) on \\\\([0,1]\\\\). P\u2019s bounded convex Lipschitz sample-loss assumptions hold on this compact set. I will put the derivation in ada/.\\n#8 [emmy/finding] Despite #5\u20136, Q Eq. (40) appears to admit a direct full-implementation proof without strict monotonicity. For each resource \\\\(k\\\\), write \\\\(P^k=\\\\sum_n p_n^k\\\\) and \\\\(z^k=\\\\sum_n x_n^k-c^k\\\\). Q\u2019s tax gives \\\\(\\\\partial_{p_n^k}U_n=-\\\\alpha(Np_n^k-P^k-z^k)/(N-1)\\\\). At a price best response, if any \\\\(p_n^k>0\\\\), then \\\\(Np_n^k=P^k+z^k>0\\\\), forcing every price positive/equal and then \\\\(z^k=0\\\\) by summation. If all prices vanish, the boundary derivative condition gives \\\\(z^k\\\\le0\\\\). Hence every NE has common \\\\(p^k\\\\ge0\\\\), feasible allocations, and \\\\(p^k z^k=0\\\\). At common prices, \\\\(\\\\partial_{x_n}t_n=\\\\alpha p\\\\), so each allocation best response maximizes \\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n\\\\). These are precisely planner KKT conditions with \\\\(\\\\lambda=\\\\alpha p\\\\), proving every NE allocation optimal for concave \\\\(V_n\\\\), even nonsmooth via subgradients. Conversely a planner primal-dual pair yields an NE (assuming a multiplier exists). Q\u2019s unique-message claim fails: \\\\(N=2,K=1,\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],c=2,V_n(x)=2x-x^2/2,\\\\alpha=1\\\\) has \\\\(x_1=x_2=1\\\\) and any common \\\\(p\\\\in[0,1]\\\\) as NE. This outcome-only theorem is the safer foundation for combining Q with P\u2019s CVaR oracle.\\n#9 [emmy/correction] Qualification to #8: the converse from planner primal-dual pair to a Nash equilibrium needs Q\u2019s \\\\(\\\\alpha\\\\)-strong concavity (or another condition making each player\u2019s objective jointly concave in \\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\)), not mere concavity. In Eq. (40), the own \\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\) cross coefficient is \\\\(\\\\alpha/(N-1)\\\\), while the own price curvature is \\\\(-\\\\alpha\\\\). Combining Q\u2019s \\\\(-\\\\alpha\\\\) curvature in \\\\(x_n\\\\) gives a negative semidefinite own Hessian for \\\\(N\\\\ge2\\\\); then the first-order conditions suffice. The forward statement, every existing NE allocates optimally, only needs concavity of the planner valuation and the price best-response identities.\\n#10 [ada/finding] Further correction: Q Eq. (40) also fails its individual-rationality claim under stated assumptions. Let \\\\(N=2,K=1,c=1,\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1],\\\\alpha=1\\\\), \\\\(V_1(x)=4x-x^2/2\\\\), \\\\(V_2(x)=x-x^2/2\\\\). The social optimum is \\\\((x_1,x_2)=(1,0)\\\\). At common price \\\\(p_1=p_2=3\\\\), all price derivatives vanish; \\\\(\\\\partial_{x_1}U_1=V_1\\\\prime(1)-3=0\\\\), \\\\(\\\\partial_{x_2}U_2=V_2\\\\prime(0)-3=-2\\\\le0\\\\), and own utilities are jointly concave, so this is an NE. Substitution into Q Eq. (40) gives \\\\(t_1=3p(x_1-c/2)=9/2\\\\), whereas \\\\(V_1(1)=7/2\\\\); hence \\\\(U_1=-1<0=V_1(0)\\\\). The IR proof\u2019s proposed zero-demand deviation also fails: holding prices and rival fixed, \\\\(t_1((0,3),(0,3))=3/2>0\\\\). This can arise from P-compatible CVaR losses \\\\(J_n(x,\\\\xi)=x^2/2-(b_n+1/4+\\\\xi)x\\\\), \\\\(b_1=4,b_2=1\\\\), \\\\(\\\\xi=\\\\pm1/4\\\\) equiprobably, tail \\\\(\\\\beta=1/2\\\\). Thus my #4 candidate theorem must drop exact/approximate IR for Eq. (40), unless a redesigned transfer rule is proved. Exact budget balance and optimal allocation still survive. I will document this counterexample in ada/.\\n#11 [emmy/objection] Further failure of Q Eq. (40): individual rationality can fail at a genuine NE. Let \\\\(N=2,K=1,\\\\mathcal X_1=\\\\mathcal X_2=[0,1],c=1,\\\\alpha=0.1\\\\), \\\\(V_1(x)=1.1x-0.05x^2\\\\), \\\\(V_2(x)=0.5x-0.05x^2\\\\). The planner optimum is \\\\((1,0)\\\\), with multiplier \\\\(\\\\lambda=1\\\\); Eq. (40) has an NE at \\\\((x_1,x_2,p_1,p_2)=(1,0,10,10)\\\\). Indeed the own payoff Hessian is \\\\(\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-0.1&0.1\\\\\\\\0.1&-0.1\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\preceq0\\\\), and at this profile price derivatives vanish, agent 1\u2019s allocation derivative is \\\\(V_1\\\\prime(1)-1=0\\\\), agent 2\u2019s is \\\\(V_2\\\\prime(0)-1=-0.5\\\\) at lower bound. Direct substitution in Eq. (40) gives \\\\(t_1=1.5\\\\), \\\\(t_2=-1.5\\\\). Thus \\\\(U_1=V_1(1)-t_1=1.05-1.5=-0.45<0=V_1(0)\\\\), contradicting Q\u2019s P4/Proposition (prop2). P-compatible stochastic losses realizing these valuations are \\\\(J_1(x,\\\\xi)=0.05x^2-(1.2+\\\\xi)x\\\\), \\\\(J_2(x,\\\\xi)=0.05x^2-(0.6+\\\\xi)x\\\\), with \\\\(\\\\xi=\\\\pm0.1\\\\) equiprobably and CVaR upper-tail level \\\\(\\\\beta=1/2\\\\); then \\\\(-\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{1/2}J_n=V_n\\\\) on \\\\([0,1]\\\\), and each sample loss is convex, bounded, Lipschitz. A Q\u2013P synthesis should target efficient allocations and budget balance first, then redesign transfers if exact IR is required.\\n#12 [ada/idea] Proposed exact repair of Q Eq. (40) IR, without changing any best response: write \\\\(\\\\kappa_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2=1+N/(N-1)^2\\\\). Direct substitution at any common-price complementary NE gives \\\\(t_n=\\\\alpha\\\\kappa_N p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\) (terms involving \\\\(p^\\\\top(\\\\sum_mx_m-c)\\\\) vanish). Add the others-only rebate \\\\(R_n(s_{-n})=\\\\alpha(\\\\kappa_N-1)(\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1))^\\\\top(\\\\bar x_{-n}-(N-1)c/N)\\\\), and define \\\\(t_n^\\\\mathrm{new}=t_n+R_n\\\\). At NE, \\\\(\\\\bar p_{-n}/(N-1)=p\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\bar x_{-n}=c-x_n\\\\) in every priced coordinate, so \\\\(t_n^\\\\mathrm{new}=\\\\alpha p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\). Hence \\\\(\\\\sum_n t_n^\\\\mathrm{new}=0\\\\) and \\\\(U_n^\\\\mathrm{new}\\\\ge V_n(0)+\\\\alpha p^\\\\top c/N\\\\ge0\\\\), since \\\\(x_n\\\\) maximizes \\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n\\\\) and \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\). The rebate uses no \\\\(s_n\\\\), so NE set and efficient allocation are unchanged. Need check the formula carefully for slack coordinates and transfer signs; this could be the constructive Q\u2013P line once paired with P\u2019s CVaR surrogate oracle.\\n#13 [emmy/idea] Transfer repair for Q Eq. (40), independent of valuation reports: at any NE, price complementarity from #8 gives \\\\(p_n=p\\\\), \\\\(\\\\lambda=\\\\alpha p\\\\), and \\\\(p^\\\\top(\\\\sum_mx_m-c)=0\\\\). Direct simplification of Eq. (40) gives \\\\(t_n=A_N\\\\lambda^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\), with \\\\(A_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2=1+N/(N-1)^2\\\\). Define an added payment depending only on others\u2019 messages, \\\\(g_n(s_{-n})=\\\\alpha N/(N-1)^3\\\\,\\\\bar p_{-n}^\\\\top[\\\\bar x_{-n}-(N-1)c/N]\\\\), and \\\\(t_n^{\\\\rm new}=t_n+g_n\\\\). Since \\\\(g_n\\\\) is independent of \\\\(s_n\\\\), the entire NE set and all best responses are unchanged. At every NE, complementarity gives \\\\(g_n=(1-A_N)\\\\lambda^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\), hence \\\\(t_n^{\\\\rm new}=\\\\lambda^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\). Thus \\\\(\\\\sum_n t_n^{\\\\rm new}=\\\\lambda^\\\\top(\\\\sum_nx_n-c)=0\\\\), and \\\\(U_n^{\\\\rm new}=V_n(x_n)-\\\\lambda^\\\\top x_n+\\\\lambda^\\\\top c/N\\\\ge V_n(0)+\\\\lambda^\\\\top c/N\\\\ge V_n(0)\\\\), because each \\\\(x_n\\\\) maximizes \\\\(V_n(x_n)-\\\\lambda^\\\\top x_n\\\\) and \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\). This appears to restore exact IR and BB without changing Q\u2019s efficient equilibrium allocations; I am checking algebra and edge cases.\\n#14 [emmy/finding] Sharper structural theorem for Q Eq. (40) and the repaired tax in #12\u201313: assume \\\\(N\\\\ge2\\\\), \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\) compact convex, \\\\(V_n\\\\) upper semicontinuous and \\\\(\\\\alpha\\\\)-strongly concave, and existence of a planner multiplier. Then the NE set is exactly \\\\(\\\\{(x^\\\\star,p_1=\\\\cdots=p_N=\\\\lambda/\\\\alpha):\\\\lambda\\\\in\\\\Lambda^\\\\star\\\\}\\\\), where \\\\(x^\\\\star\\\\) is the unique welfare-maximizing allocation and \\\\(\\\\Lambda^\\\\star\\\\) is the (possibly non-singleton) optimal multiplier set. Necessity follows from price best responses and allocation best responses (#8). Sufficiency follows because each player\u2019s payoff is jointly concave in \\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\): Q\u2019s strong curvature gives own Hessian bounded by \\\\(\\\\alpha\\\\begin{psmallmatrix}-I&I/(N-1)\\\\\\\\I/(N-1)&-I\\\\end{psmallmatrix}\\\\preceq0\\\\), so KKT conditions imply a global best response. The others-only repair preserves this set and proves BB+IR for every equilibrium, including nonunique prices. Thus the correct dynamic target is the equilibrium set or unique allocation, not a unique message profile.\\n#15 [emmy/finding] Quantitative Q\u2013P synthesis conditional on solving the learning step: for each agent let \\\\(C_n(x)=\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{\\\\beta_n}J_n(x,\\\\xi_n)\\\\), \\\\(H_{n,s}(x)=\\\\mathbb E[\\\\widehat C_{n,s}(x)]\\\\) for a fresh size-\\\\(s_n\\\\) empirical CVaR batch, and normalized valuations \\\\(V_n(x)=u_n(x)-C_n(x)-[u_n(0)-C_n(0)]\\\\), \\\\(\\\\widetilde V_n(x)=u_n(x)-H_{n,s}(x)-[u_n(0)-H_{n,s}(0)]\\\\). If P\u2019s sample losses are convex, \\\\(L_n\\\\)-Lipschitz and bounded by \\\\(U_n\\\\), and \\\\(u_n\\\\) is \\\\(\\\\alpha\\\\)-strongly concave, both valuations are \\\\(\\\\alpha\\\\)-strongly concave. P Appendix CVaR-stability lemma plus its DKW integration gives \\\\(\\\\sup_x|H_{n,s}(x)-C_n(x)|\\\\le\\\\kappa_n:=U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}/(\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\); this is a deterministic bound on the expected surrogate, with no uniform-over-samples claim. Thus \\\\(\\\\sup_x|V_n-\\\\widetilde V_n|\\\\le\\\\varepsilon_n:=2\\\\kappa_n\\\\). Run Q Eq. (40) with the others-only transfer repair #13 for the surrogate game. At any surrogate NE \\\\(\\\\widetilde s\\\\), the true planner welfare gap is at most \\\\(2\\\\sum_n\\\\varepsilon_n=4\\\\sum_n\\\\kappa_n\\\\); true unilateral gain from any deviation is at most \\\\(2\\\\varepsilon_n=4\\\\kappa_n\\\\); total payments are exactly zero; and true utility is at least \\\\(-\\\\varepsilon_n=-2\\\\kappa_n\\\\) relative to the normalized outside option. Strong concavity further gives \\\\(\\\\|\\\\widetilde x-x^\\\\star\\\\|\\\\le2\\\\sqrt{\\\\sum_n\\\\varepsilon_n/\\\\alpha}\\\\). These are direct two-sided value perturbation bounds plus #14, not consequences of P\u2019s consensus theorem. If \\\\(J_n(0,\\\\xi)=0\\\\) a.s., normalization adds no extra error and every factor \\\\(2\\\\) from \\\\(\\\\varepsilon_n=2\\\\kappa_n\\\\) drops.\\n#16 [emmy/finding] Narrow prior-work check (not a novelty claim): searched `site:arxiv.org risk averse CVaR mechanism design budget balance Nash implementation resource allocation`, `site:arxiv.org conditional value at risk mechanism design distributed resource allocation Nash equilibrium budget balanced`, `site:arxiv.org zeroth order CVaR generalized Nash equilibrium stochastic aggregative game`, `\\\"CVaR\\\" \\\"budget balanced\\\" mechanism design resource allocation`, and `\\\"CVaR\\\" \\\"Nash implementation\\\" mechanism design`. Relevant adjacent papers found: Dahlin\u2013Jain, https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.06119, includes CVaR in a two-stage market but explicitly assumes nonstrategic agents for its market mechanism (abstract); Du\u2013Meng, https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06023, gives distributed learning for stochastic aggregative games under expectation-valued costs and strong monotonicity (abstract), assumptions unavailable for Q\u2019s Eq. (40) because #5\u20136. These do not establish novelty of our repaired-tax plus CVaR-oracle direction; a fuller literature review remains.\\n#17 [ada/correction] My #4 overstated the true-game regret and assumed IR from the original tax. After the others-only repair #12\u201313, the bounds sharpen because valuation normalization constants cancel in comparisons: if \\\\(\\\\sup_x|\\\\widetilde C_n(x)-C_n(x)|\\\\le h_n\\\\), then a surrogate NE has true unilateral regret \\\\(\\\\le2h_n\\\\) and true planner loss \\\\(\\\\le2\\\\sum_nh_n\\\\), although \\\\(\\\\sup_x|\\\\widetilde V_n-V_n|\\\\le2h_n\\\\). True individual utility is \\\\(\\\\ge-2h_n\\\\), and transfers balance exactly. With P\u2019s fixed-sample expected empirical CVaR plus smoothing, \\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}/(\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\) under oracle access on a \\\\(\\\\delta_n\\\\)-neighborhood. The precise derivation, Q counterexamples, tax repair, and remaining dynamic gap are in ada/pair_note.md; both style checks pass. This refines #15\u2019s generic factors by exploiting cancellation, and includes smoothing needed for Q-style gradient play.\\n#18 [emmy/correction] Sharpening #15 after cross-checking ada/pair_note.md: the normalization constants cancel when comparing two allocations or a unilateral deviation. Let \\\\(d_n(x)=H_{n,s}(x)-C_n(x)\\\\), with \\\\(|d_n(x)|\\\\le\\\\kappa_n\\\\). Then \\\\((V_n-\\\\widetilde V_n)(x)=d_n(x)-d_n(0)\\\\), so differences between two decisions have magnitude at most \\\\(2\\\\kappa_n\\\\), not \\\\(4\\\\kappa_n\\\\). Consequently the true welfare gap at a surrogate NE is \\\\(\\\\le2\\\\sum_n\\\\kappa_n\\\\), true unilateral regret is \\\\(\\\\le2\\\\kappa_n\\\\), and strong concavity gives \\\\(\\\\|\\\\widetilde x-x^\\\\star\\\\|\\\\le2\\\\sqrt{\\\\sum_n\\\\kappa_n/\\\\alpha}\\\\). The true IR shortfall remains \\\\(\\\\le2\\\\kappa_n\\\\), since it compares normalized utility with zero. Ada\u2019s note has these sharper factors; #15 remains valid but loose. The same cancellation holds with P\u2019s smoothing term \\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+\\\\kappa_n\\\\) wherever its queries are feasible.\\n#19 [ada/objection] An additional dynamic-evidence mismatch in Q: Theorem theorem4 (inputs/Q.tex around lines 518\u2013523) assumes \\\\(Q_i=\\\\lceil C_b/\\\\eta^{2(i+1)}\\\\rceil\\\\) with \\\\(0<\\\\eta<1\\\\), so sample size grows geometrically. Its numerical section (around lines 678\u2013685) instead says \\\\(Q_i=\\\\lceil\\\\widetilde\\\\eta^{i+1}\\\\rceil\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\widetilde\\\\eta=0.96\\\\), which equals \\\\(1\\\\) at every iteration. Thus the plotted trajectories do not instantiate the theorem\u2019s sampling regime as written. This reinforces that a Q\u2013P paper should prove its own learning statement; P\u2019s fixed-batch result targets a shared decision and cannot certify Q\u2019s game.\\n#20 [emmy/finding] Scope and remaining gap for a publication: I sharpened the pair question from \u201cis there a connection?\u201d to \u201ccan an efficient, balanced, individually rational resource-allocation mechanism be learned from black-box CVaR losses with a finite-sample economic guarantee?\u201d The static answer is the repaired-tax theorem (#14, #18; also ada/pair_note.md). P\u2019s objective is the sum/average of individual CVaRs (P Preliminaries and Appendix remark), so this does not optimize \\\\(\\\\operatorname{CVaR}(\\\\sum_nJ_n)\\\\) in general. A full paper still needs a decentralized zeroth-order or primal-dual learning theorem for Q\u2019s heterogeneous allocations, with feasible perturbation queries near nonnegative boundaries, and convergence to the equilibrium set despite nonunique prices and lack of Q\u2019s strict monotonicity. P\u2019s exact-consensus theorem solves a different shared-decision problem and cannot supply that step. Fixed fresh-batch expected empirical CVaR is a deterministic surrogate; its value bound alone does not prove that an implementable strategic learning rule reaches its equilibrium. Q also allows interim capacity violations because its allocation rule is \\\\(y_n=x_n\\\\) and feasibility emerges only at equilibrium. The prior-work search in #16 was narrow, so novelty is unresolved.\\n#21 [emmy/ready] ready at #20\\n#22 [ada/finding] Final independent account is in ada/pair_note.md (style-gates and local artifact ban both pass). Q Eq. (40) has efficient equilibrium allocations but its claimed strict P1 LMI is incompatible with P2(i); prices need not be unique, and original transfers can violate IR (#5, #7, #10). The others-only rebate in #12 restores exact equilibrium budget balance and IR without changing best responses; I checked the general coefficient algebra for several \\\\(N\\\\) and the explicit IR counterexample numerically. Pairing that repaired tax with P\u2019s fixed fresh-batch expected empirical CVaR plus smoothing yields a deterministic surrogate with \\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}/(\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\) pointwise value error, hence true welfare loss \\\\(\\\\le2\\\\sum_nh_n\\\\), true unilateral regret \\\\(\\\\le2h_n\\\\), and true utility \\\\(\\\\ge-2h_n\\\\) at any surrogate NE, conditional on an oracle neighborhood and a planner multiplier. Exact dynamic convergence from P\u2019s samples remains unproved: P\u2019s consensus theorem addresses a shared decision, whereas Q has separate coupled allocations and nonunique prices. Emmy\u2019s #20 agrees on this boundary. No claim of publication novelty follows from this pair alone.\\n#23 [ada/ready] ready at #22\\n#24 [emmy/finding] Reopened after ada #22 and independently checked its core algebra against Q Eq. (40), lines 412\u2013422, and P Appendix CVaR/DKW bound, lines 1287\u20131315. At a common-price complementary equilibrium, Q\u2019s original tax is \\\\(t_n=\\\\alpha[1+N/(N-1)^2]p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\). The others-only rebate in #13 therefore leaves every best response intact and reduces the equilibrium tax to \\\\(\\\\alpha p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\), giving exact balance and participation when \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\) and valuations are normalized. P\u2019s \\\\(2U/\\\\beta\\\\) CVaR CDF bound and integrated DKW bound yield \\\\(U\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}/(\\\\beta\\\\sqrt{s})\\\\) in expected absolute value at each point; because this constant is point-independent, the expected empirical surrogate obeys the deterministic bound used by ada. The sharper \\\\(2h_n\\\\) regret and \\\\(2\\\\sum_nh_n\\\\) welfare bounds in #17/#22 follow by canceling valuation-normalization constants. I found no new obstruction to the static theorem. Dynamic convergence, feasible perturbation queries, multiplier existence, and literature novelty remain unresolved; P\u2019s common-decision consensus theorem does not discharge them.\\n#25 [emmy/ready] ready at #24\\n#26 [ada/finding] Final source cross-check: Q Eq. (40), inputs/Q.tex lines 410\u2013422, gives the price derivative and common-price equilibrium tax in ada/pair_note.md; Q P1(ii) and P2(i), lines 200\u2013250, still contradict along a common-price direction. P Assumptions Lipschitz/convex, inputs/P.tex lines 495\u2013511, and its Appendix CVaR/DKW estimate, lines 1287\u20131315, support the expected empirical surrogate and pointwise error \\\\(h_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}/(\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\), provided queries are possible on the full perturbation neighborhood. The rebate is independent of agent \\\\(n\\\\) messages, so the Q equilibrium set stays fixed; it yields true welfare gap \\\\(\\\\le 2\\\\sum_n h_n\\\\) and unilateral regret \\\\(\\\\le 2h_n\\\\) by cancellation of normalization constants. I found no new obstruction to this static theorem. Implementable convergence, feasible perturbation queries, multiplier existence, and novelty remain unresolved; P Algorithm 1 uses consensus over one common decision, not Q heterogeneous coupled allocations.\\n#27 [ada/ready] ready at #26\\n#28 [verifier/review] ITERATE: The connection is promising, but the note\u2019s payment identity is wrong for general \\\\(N\\\\). Expanding Q\u2019s Eq. (40) at equilibrium gives \\\\(t_n=(N+1)\\\\lambda^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)/(N-1)^2\\\\); the proposed rebate then gives \\\\((t_n+g_n)=\\\\lambda^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)/(N-1)^2\\\\), rather than the claimed coefficient \\\\(1\\\\). The ledger repeats the incorrect identity, so the transfer argument needs a fresh general-\\\\(N\\\\) check before the static result is drafted. Action: Recompute the equilibrium transfers for arbitrary \\\\(N\\\\), then verify budget balance and individual rationality using the corrected coefficient.\\n#29 [emmy/correction] Verifier #28 is incorrect for Q Eq. (40), lines 412\u2013422: it omits the third term\u2019s factor \\\\(N/(N-1)\\\\). At common price \\\\(p_m=p\\\\), write \\\\(z=\\\\sum_mx_m-c\\\\) and \\\\(d_n=x_n-c/N\\\\). The price-quadratic terms cancel, the third term is \\\\(\\\\alpha Np^\\\\top d_n/(N-1)\\\\), and the final term is \\\\(\\\\alpha[p^\\\\top d_n-p^\\\\top z]/(N-1)^2\\\\). Thus \\\\(t_n/\\\\alpha=[N/(N-1)+1/(N-1)^2]p^\\\\top d_n-[1/(N-1)+1/(N-1)^2]p^\\\\top z\\\\). At complementary equilibrium \\\\(p^\\\\top z=0\\\\), the coefficient is \\\\(\\\\kappa_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2\\\\), as in #12\u201313 and ada/pair_note.md. The rebate in #13 contributes \\\\(-\\\\alpha Np^\\\\top d_n/(N-1)^2\\\\), leaving \\\\(t_n^{\\\\rm new}=\\\\alpha p^\\\\top d_n\\\\), so balance and IR remain valid. For \\\\(N=3,c=3,x=(2,1,0),p=1,n=1,\\\\alpha=1\\\\), Eq. (40) gives \\\\(t_1=7/4\\\\), rebate \\\\(-3/4\\\\), and revised transfer \\\\(1\\\\); #28 predicts \\\\(1\\\\) for the original tax and is contradicted directly.\\nlatest substantive entry: 29\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The ledger now has a verifier objection to the transfer coefficient and a correction from emmy. I\u2019m checking Eq. (40) directly for general \\\\(N\\\\), then I\u2019ll update the ledger with my independent conclusion.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat ada/pair_note.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat ada/pair_note.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# A risk aware allocation mechanism suggested by Q and P\\n\\n## Question and scope\\n\\nI sharpened the assigned question to this one: can Q's strategic resource\\nallocation game use P's sampled CVaR oracle while retaining allocation\\nefficiency, balanced transfers, and participation guarantees? The answer\\nrequires a correction to Q's advertised tax and a weaker claim about price\\nuniqueness. No prior work search was performed.\\n\\nQ's planner allocates separate \\\\(x_n\\\\) subject to\\n\\\\(\\\\sum_n x_n\\\\le c\\\\) ([Q, lines 105\u2013134](../inputs/Q.tex)). P's agents\\ninstead hold copies of a common decision and average them through a\\ncommunication graph ([P, lines 270\u2013276](../inputs/P.tex) and its\\n\\\\(\\\\texttt{eq:update y}\\\\)). Its consensus iteration therefore cannot be\\ninserted into Q's allocation game. The transferable part is P's local\\nempirical CVaR estimator and its fixed sample surrogate\\n([P, lines 374\u2013490](../inputs/P.tex), [P, lines 854\u2013880](../inputs/P.tex)).\\n\\n## What Q's tax actually proves\\n\\nLet \\\\(N\\\\ge2\\\\), and take Q's tax in \\\\(\\\\texttt{eq40}\\\\)\\n([Q, lines 410\u2013428](../inputs/Q.tex)). Write\\n\\\\(z=\\\\sum_m x_m-c\\\\) and \\\\(P=\\\\sum_m p_m\\\\). For any resource coordinate,\\nthe price derivative of agent \\\\(n\\\\)'s payoff is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n\\n=-\\\\frac{\\\\alpha}{N-1}(Np_n-P-z).\\n\\\\]\\nAt a price best response, either every agent has positive, equal prices\\nand \\\\(z=0\\\\), or every price is zero and \\\\(z\\\\le0\\\\). Apply this argument\\ncoordinatewise. Thus every Nash equilibrium has a common price \\\\(p\\\\),\\nfeasible allocations, and \\\\(p^\\\\top z=0\\\\). At common prices,\\n\\\\(\\\\nabla_{x_n}t_n=\\\\alpha p\\\\), so every allocation best response solves\\n\\\\(\\\\max_{x_n\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n}\\\\{V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n\\\\}\\\\).\\nThese are the planner's primal and dual conditions. Hence every Nash\\nequilibrium has a welfare maximizing allocation. Strong concavity makes\\nthat allocation unique. Conversely, a planner primal and dual solution\\ngives a Nash equilibrium if a multiplier exists: Q's own payoff is\\njointly concave in \\\\((x_n,p_n)\\\\), because its own Hessian is bounded\\nabove by\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n-\\\\alpha I & \\\\alpha I/(N-1)\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\alpha I/(N-1)&-\\\\alpha I\\n\\\\end{pmatrix}\\\\preceq0.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nQ's strict uniqueness LMI is impossible together with its\\nimplementation LMI. Q's \\\\(\\\\texttt{P2(i)}\\\\) requires\\n\\\\(\\\\sum_m A_{nm}^n=0\\\\) for every \\\\(n\\\\)\\n([Q, lines 240\u2013251](../inputs/Q.tex)). For a nonzero direction \\\\(v\\\\)\\nthat changes all prices by the same \\\\(h\\\\) and leaves allocations fixed,\\nthe price blocks of Q's matrix \\\\(\\\\Psi\\\\) give\\n\\\\[\\nv^\\\\top(\\\\Psi+\\\\Psi^\\\\top)v\\n=-2\\\\sum_n h^\\\\top\\\\bigl(\\\\sum_m A_{nm}^n\\\\bigr)h=0.\\n\\\\]\\nThis contradicts Q's \\\\(\\\\texttt{P1(ii)}\\\\), which demands the expression\\nbe at most \\\\(-\\\\upsilon\\\\|v\\\\|^2\\\\) for \\\\(\\\\upsilon>0\\\\)\\n([Q, lines 200\u2013213](../inputs/Q.tex)). It does not negate the\\nallocation result above.\\n\\nThere can be genuinely multiple Nash prices. Let\\n\\\\(N=2\\\\), \\\\(K=1\\\\), \\\\(c=2\\\\), \\\\(\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1]\\\\), \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\), and\\n\\\\(V_n(x)=2x-x^2/2\\\\). For every \\\\(p\\\\in[0,1]\\\\),\\n\\\\((x_1,p_1,x_2,p_2)=(1,p,1,p)\\\\) is a Nash equilibrium.\\nIndeed, Q's tax gives\\n\\\\(\\\\partial_{x_n}U_n=V_n'(x_n)+p_n-2p_{-n}=1-p\\\\ge0\\\\)\\nat the upper allocation bound, and\\n\\\\(\\\\partial_{p_n}U_n=-p_n+p_{-n}+x_1+x_2-c=0\\\\).\\nThe own payoff Hessian is\\n\\\\(\\\\left(\\\\begin{smallmatrix}-1&1\\\\\\\\1&-1\\\\end{smallmatrix}\\\\right)\\\\preceq0\\\\),\\nso these conditions establish global best responses. The allocation is\\nunique; prices are not.\\n\\n## A participation failure and a transfer repair\\n\\nQ's same tax can fail individual rationality. Set\\n\\\\(N=2\\\\), \\\\(K=1\\\\), \\\\(c=1\\\\), \\\\(\\\\mathcal X_n=[0,1]\\\\), \\\\(\\\\alpha=1\\\\),\\n\\\\(V_1(x)=4x-x^2/2\\\\), and \\\\(V_2(x)=x-x^2/2\\\\).\\nThe profile \\\\((x_1,p_1,x_2,p_2)=(1,3,0,3)\\\\) is a Nash equilibrium by\\nthe price condition above and the allocation derivatives\\n\\\\(V_1'(1)-3=0\\\\) and \\\\(V_2'(0)-3=-2\\\\). Joint concavity makes these\\nsufficient. Q's transfer is \\\\(t_1=9/2\\\\), while \\\\(V_1(1)=7/2\\\\).\\nConsequently \\\\(U_1=-1<0=V_1(0)\\\\), contrary to Q's participation\\nclaim in \\\\(\\\\texttt{IR\\\\_prop}\\\\)\\n([Q, lines 386\u2013407](../inputs/Q.tex)).\\n\\nThe excess charge can be removed without changing anyone's incentives.\\nDefine \\\\(\\\\kappa_N=(N^2-N+1)/(N-1)^2=1+N/(N-1)^2\\\\). At every\\nequilibrium, direct substitution into Q's tax yields\\n\\\\[\\nt_n=\\\\alpha\\\\kappa_N p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N).\\n\\\\]\\nAdd this term, which depends only on other agents' messages:\\n\\\\[\\nR_n(s_{-n})=\\n\\\\alpha(\\\\kappa_N-1)\\n\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\bar p_{-n}}{N-1}\\\\right)^\\\\top\\n\\\\left(\\\\bar x_{-n}-\\\\frac{N-1}{N}c\\\\right),\\n\\\\qquad t_n^{\\\\mathrm{new}}=t_n+R_n.\\n\\\\]\\nBest responses and the equilibrium set are unchanged. At an equilibrium,\\nthe revised transfer simplifies to\\n\\\\(t_n^{\\\\mathrm{new}}=\\\\alpha p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\). Therefore\\n\\\\(\\\\sum_n t_n^{\\\\mathrm{new}}=0\\\\), and if \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nU_n^{\\\\mathrm{new}}\\n=\\\\bigl(V_n(x_n)-\\\\alpha p^\\\\top x_n\\\\bigr)\\n+\\\\alpha p^\\\\top c/N\\n\\\\ge V_n(0)+\\\\alpha p^\\\\top c/N\\\\ge0.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is exact balance and participation at every equilibrium, including\\nthe multiple price equilibria. The argument uses the corrected transfer,\\nnot Q's stated individual rationality LMI.\\n\\n## Combining the repair with P's CVaR oracle\\n\\nFor each agent, let the sample loss \\\\(J_n(x,\\\\xi)\\\\) be convex,\\n\\\\(L_n\\\\)-Lipschitz, and bounded by \\\\(U_n\\\\) on a \\\\(\\\\delta_n\\\\)-neighborhood\\nof \\\\(\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\). Assume that \\\\(0\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_n\\\\) and this\\nneighborhood admits counterfactual oracle queries. Let the upper-tail\\nrisk level be \\\\(\\\\beta_n\\\\in(0,1)\\\\), and set\\n\\\\[\\nC_n(x)=\\\\operatorname{CVaR}_{\\\\beta_n}J_n(x,\\\\xi),\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widetilde C_n(x)=\\n\\\\mathbb E_{\\\\nu,\\\\xi^{1:s_n}}\\n\\\\widehat{\\\\operatorname{CVaR}}_{\\\\beta_n}\\nJ_n(x+\\\\delta_n\\\\nu,\\\\xi^{1:s_n}),\\n\\\\]\\nwhere \\\\(\\\\nu\\\\) is uniform on the unit ball and the fresh batch size\\n\\\\(s_n\\\\) is fixed across iterations. Define the normalized true and\\nsurrogate valuations by\\n\\\\[\\nV_n^0(x)=C_n(0)-C_n(x)-\\\\gamma\\\\|x\\\\|^2/2,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\widetilde V_n(x)=\\n\\\\widetilde C_n(0)-\\\\widetilde C_n(x)-\\\\gamma\\\\|x\\\\|^2/2.\\n\\\\]\\nP's convexity and smoothing arguments make \\\\(\\\\widetilde V_n\\\\)\\ndifferentiable and \\\\(\\\\gamma\\\\)-strongly concave\\n([P, lines 374\u2013405](../inputs/P.tex),\\n[P, lines 495\u2013510](../inputs/P.tex)). Use Q's tax with\\n\\\\(\\\\alpha=\\\\gamma\\\\) and the rebate above. Provided a planner multiplier\\nexists, the resulting game has an equilibrium, every equilibrium\\nallocates the surrogate planner optimum, and its revised transfers are\\nexactly balanced and individually rational for surrogate preferences.\\nThe price may still be nonunique.\\n\\nP's CVaR distribution bound and DKW calculation\\n([P, lines 1284\u20131328](../inputs/P.tex)) imply, at every fixed query\\npoint,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E|\\\\widehat C_{n,s_n}(x)-C_n(x)|\\n\\\\le\\\\frac{U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}}{\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n}}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe constant is independent of the point. Together with P's\\n\\\\(L_n\\\\delta_n\\\\) smoothing bound, this gives the uniform deterministic\\nvalue estimate\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\widetilde C_n(x)-C_n(x)|\\\\le h_n,\\n\\\\qquad\\nh_n=L_n\\\\delta_n+\\n\\\\frac{U_n\\\\sqrt{2\\\\pi}}{\\\\beta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n}}.\\n\\\\]\\nLet \\\\(x^0\\\\) minimize the true total regularized CVaR on Q's coupled\\nfeasible set, and let \\\\(\\\\widetilde x\\\\) be any equilibrium allocation\\nof the surrogate game. Comparing the two objective values gives\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sum_n\\\\left[C_n(\\\\widetilde x_n)+\\\\gamma\\\\|\\\\widetilde x_n\\\\|^2/2\\\\right]\\n-\\\\sum_n\\\\left[C_n(x_n^0)+\\\\gamma\\\\|x_n^0\\\\|^2/2\\\\right]\\n\\\\le 2\\\\sum_n h_n,\\n\\\\qquad\\n\\\\|\\\\widetilde x-x^0\\\\|\\n\\\\le2\\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{\\\\sum_n h_n}{\\\\gamma}}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe second inequality uses strong convexity and convex feasibility.\\nAt the same equilibrium, a unilateral deviation in the true CVaR game\\ncan improve an agent's utility by at most \\\\(2h_n\\\\), while the agent's\\ntrue utility is at least \\\\(-2h_n\\\\). The revised transfers remain\\nexactly balanced. These bounds compare value functions, so their\\nsampling term is proportional to \\\\(s_n^{-1/2}\\\\); P's gradient error\\nbound instead contains \\\\(1/(\\\\delta_n\\\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\)\\n([P, lines 559\u2013568](../inputs/P.tex)). This is a static comparison\\nof surrogate equilibria, not an improved finite-time algorithm bound.\\n\\nThe sample losses in the participation counterexample can be made\\ngenuinely stochastic and P-compatible: take\\n\\\\(J_n(x,\\\\xi)=x^2/2-(b_n+1/4+\\\\xi)x\\\\), with\\n\\\\(b_1=4\\\\), \\\\(b_2=1\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\xi=\\\\pm1/4\\\\) equiprobably.\\nFor \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1]\\\\), the upper-tail CVaR at \\\\(\\\\beta=1/2\\\\) is\\n\\\\(x^2/2-b_nx\\\\), so its negative is exactly \\\\(V_n\\\\) above.\\n\\n## Open work\\n\\nThe main missing result is a learning rule that reaches a surrogate\\nequilibrium from P's sampled loss values in Q's strategic allocation\\ngame. P proves convergence for copies of one shared decision, while Q's\\nadvertised nonexpansive proximal response and strict LMI certificate\\ncannot be imported directly. A viable next step is a primal and dual\\nalgorithm with the corrected transfer and fixed sample surrogate, with\\nits convergence proved under explicit oracle geometry and multiplier\\nassumptions. Q's simulation also uses a constant sample size as written,\\n\\\\(Q_i=\\\\lceil0.96^{i+1}\\\\rceil=1\\\\), while its stated convergence theorem\\nrequires a geometrically increasing size\\n([Q, lines 518\u2013523 and 679\u2013682](../inputs/Q.tex)).\\nThe static theorem and the counterexamples above are\\nderivations from the supplied papers, not a claim of literature novelty.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '480,530p' inputs/P.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '480,530p' inputs/P.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Using the CVaR gradient estimate in \\\\eqref{eq:estimated gradient}, agent $i$ updates its decision by\\n\\\\begin{align}\\nx_{k+1}^i &= \\\\mathcal{P}_{\\\\mathcal{X}_\\\\delta} [y_{k}^i - \\\\eta_k \\\\hat{g}_k^i]. \\\\label{eq:gradient descent}\\n\\\\end{align}   \\nHere, $x_0^i\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_\\\\delta$ is the initial decision and $\\\\eta_k>0$ is the step size.\\nNote that \\n$  \\\\mathcal{X}_\\\\delta  \\\\oplus \\\\delta_i  \\\\mathbb{B} =  \\\\big(1-\\\\frac{\\\\delta_{\\\\max}}{r}\\\\big)\\\\mathcal{X} \\\\oplus \\\\delta_i   \\\\mathbb{B} = \\\\big(1-\\\\frac{\\\\delta_{\\\\max}}{r}\\\\big)\\\\mathcal{X} \\\\oplus \\\\frac{\\\\delta_i}{r} r   \\\\mathbb{B}  \\\\subseteq \\\\big(1-\\\\frac{\\\\delta_i}{r}\\\\big)\\\\mathcal{X} \\\\oplus \\\\frac{\\\\delta_i}{r} \\\\mathcal{X} = \\\\mathcal{X}.  $  \\nHence, by induction, $x_k^i\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_\\\\delta\\\\subset\\\\mathcal X$ for every agent and iteration. \\nConvexity of $\\\\mathcal X_\\\\delta$ and row stochasticity of $W_k$ further imply that $y_k^i\\\\in\\\\mathcal X_\\\\delta$, \\nand the construction above therefore guarantees $\\\\hat y_k^i\\\\in\\\\mathcal X$.\\n %\\n\\\\section{Convergence analysis}\\\\label{sec:analysis}\\n\\nThis section analyzes Algorithm~\\\\ref{alg:zeroth-order}. Section~\\\\ref{sec:iteration} derives the one-step recursion, Section~\\\\ref{sec:ergodic} establishes finite-time and asymptotic guarantees for the weighted ergodic iterate, and Section~\\\\ref{sec:last iterate} proves ordinary last-iterate convergence under fixed per-agent sample sizes.\\n \\nWe impose the following assumptions, which are standard in risk-averse optimization~\\\\cite{cardoso2019risk,wang2022risk}.\\n\\\\begin{assumption}\\\\label{assumption:Lipschitz}\\nFor every $i\\\\in[m]$ and $\\\\xi^i\\\\in\\\\Xi_i$, the function $J^i(\\\\cdot,\\\\xi^i)$ is $L_i$-Lipschitz continuous on $\\\\mathcal X$. Moreover, there exists $U_i>0$ such that $|J^i(x,\\\\xi^i)|\\\\le U_i$ for all $(x,\\\\xi^i)\\\\in\\\\mathcal X\\\\times\\\\Xi_i$.\\n\\\\end{assumption}\\n\\\\begin{assumption}\\\\label{assumption:convex}\\nFor every $i\\\\in[m]$ and $\\\\xi^i\\\\in\\\\Xi_i$, the function $J^i(\\\\cdot,\\\\xi^i)$ is convex on $\\\\mathcal X$.\\n\\\\end{assumption}\\n\\nWe first present two properties of the CVaR objective and its smoothed approximation; see, e.g.,~\\\\cite{cardoso2019risk}.  \\nThe proofs can be seen, e.g., in \\\\cite{cardoso2019risk}. \\n\\\\begin{lemma}[Lipschitzness]\\\\label{lemma:smoothed cvar Lipschitz} \\nUnder Assumption~\\\\ref{assumption:Lipschitz}, both $C^i$ and $C_\\\\delta^i$ are $L_i$-Lipschitz continuous on $\\\\mathcal X$, and $|C_\\\\delta^i(x)-C^i(x)|\\\\le\\\\delta_iL_i$ for every $x\\\\in\\\\mathcal X$ and $i\\\\in\\\\mathcal V$. \\n\\\\end{lemma} \\n\\\\begin{lemma}[Convexity]\\\\label{lemma:cvar convex}\\n  %\\nGiven Assumption \\\\ref{assumption:convex}, $C^i(x)$ and $C_\\\\delta^i(x)$ are both convex in $x$, for $i\\\\in\\\\mathcal{V}$. \\n\\\\end{lemma} \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\\\subsection{Iteration relation}\\\\label{sec:iteration}\\n\\nThis subsection bounds the network disagreement and finite-sample CVaR error and then incorporates both terms into a one-step recursion.\\n\\nDefine the global average of the decision state as \\n$$\\\\bar{x}_k = \\\\frac{1}{m}\\\\sum_{i=1}^m x_{k}^i.$$ \\nBecause $|J^i|\\\\le U_i$ and $\\\\|u_k^i\\\\|=1$, the estimator in~\\\\eqref{eq:estimated gradient} satisfies\\n\\\\begin{align}\\\\label{eq:gradient bound}\\n\\\\|\\\\hat{g}_k^i\\\\| &= \\\\norm{ \\\\frac{d}{\\\\delta_i}  \\\\widehat{C}_k^i(\\\\hat{y}_k^i) u_k^i }  \\\\le \\\\frac{dU_i}{\\\\delta_i}     .\\n\\\\end{align}\\nTo simplify notation, we denote $G_i = \\\\frac{dU_i}{\\\\delta_i}$ for all $ i \\\\in \\\\mathcal{V}$, and $G_{\\\\max} := \\\\max_{i\\\\in\\\\mathcal{V}} G_i$.   \\nThe following lemma bounds the network disagreement error driven by the doubly stochastic weight matrices.\\n\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\cite[Lemma 4.1]{sundhar2010distributed}\\n\\\\label{lemma:disagreement}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1275,1325p' inputs/P.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1275,1325p' inputs/P.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"whereas the distributed finite-time bound also contains graph-dependent constants arising from network disagreement. \\nTo the best of our knowledge, \\nthis is the first study of distributed stochastic convex optimization with CVaR objectives over networks. The simulations illustrate the efficacy of the method. %\\n\\n\\\\section{Appendix}\\\\label{sec:appendix}\\nThis appendix collects auxiliary CVaR and consensus results and provides the proofs omitted from the main text. \\n \\\\begin{remark} \\n The formulation allows each agent to have an individual risk level $\\\\alpha_i$. If all agents instead share a common level $\\\\alpha$, subadditivity of CVaR gives ${\\\\rm CVaR}_\\\\alpha[\\\\sum_{i=1}^mJ^i]\\\\le\\\\sum_{i=1}^m{\\\\rm CVaR}_\\\\alpha[J^i]$.\\nThe equality holds if and only if the random variables $J_1, J_2, \\\\dots, J_m$ are positively correlated and move entirely in tandem. A trivial special case is that the random variables are exactly identical.  \\nIn quantitative finance, the difference $ \\\\sum_{i=1}^m \\\\text{CVaR}_\\\\alpha(J^i) - \\\\text{CVaR}_\\\\alpha\\\\left(\\\\sum_{i=1}^m J^i\\\\right)$ is known as the diversification price, which represents the cost the system incurs to enable distributed computation \\\\cite{mcneil2015quantitative}.\\n\\\\end{remark}\\n\\nThe following lemma bounds the difference between two CVaR values in terms of the corresponding distribution functions. \\nIt will be used to control the finite-sample gradient-estimation error.\\n\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\cite[Lemma 3]{wang2022risk}\\\\label{lemma:cvar-estimation error bound} \\nLet $F$ and $G$ be the cumulative distribution functions of two random variables supported on $[-U,U]$. \\nGiven a risk level $\\\\alpha$, we have \\n\\\\begin{equation*} \\n    \\\\Big|\\\\text{CVaR}_\\\\alpha[F] - \\\\text{CVaR}_\\\\alpha [G] \\\\Big| \\\\le \\\\frac{2U}{\\\\alpha} \\\\sup_{x} \\\\big|F(x)-G(x)\\\\big|. \\n\\\\end{equation*} \\n\\\\end{lemma}\\n\\\\textit{Proof of Lemma~\\\\ref{lemma:cvar gradient error}.}\\nWe bound the aggregate conditional error $\\\\sum_{i=1}^m\\\\mathbb E[\\\\|e_k^i\\\\|\\\\mid\\\\mathcal F_k]$. Because $\\\\hat y_k^i$ depends on the current perturbation, define $\\\\mathcal H_k:=\\\\mathcal F_k\\\\vee\\\\sigma(u_k^1,\\\\ldots,u_k^m)$.\\nThe Dvoretzky--Kiefer--Wolfowitz inequality~\\\\cite{dvoretzky1956asymptotic} gives, for every $t\\\\ge0$,\\n\\\\begin{equation*}\\n    \\\\mathbb{P}\\\\left\\\\{ \\\\sup_{z} |\\\\hat{P}_k^i(z;\\\\hat{y}_k^i) - P_k^i(z;\\\\hat{y}_k^i)| \\\\ge t | \\\\mathcal{H}_k \\\\right\\\\}  \\\\le   2e^{-2s_k^it^2}, \\n\\\\end{equation*}\\nfor all $i$. \\nTherefore, \\\\begin{align*}\\n& \\\\mathbb{E} \\\\brk{\\\\sup_{z} |\\\\hat{P}_k^i(z;\\\\hat{y}_k^i) - P_k^i(z;\\\\hat{y}_k^i)|  \\\\big| \\\\mathcal{H}_k }   \\\\nonumber\\\\\\\\\\n&\\\\le \\\\int_{t=0}^\\\\infty 2  e^{-2s_k^it^2} {\\\\rm d} t = \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2s_k^i}}.\\n\\\\end{align*}\\nCombining this estimate with Lemma~\\\\ref{lemma:cvar-estimation error bound} yields\\n\\\\begin{align}\\\\label{eq: cvar error}\\n    \\\\norm{ \\\\widehat{C}_k^i(\\\\hat{y}_k^i) - C^i(\\\\hat{y}_k^i)} \\\\!\\\\le\\\\!  \\\\frac{2U_i}{\\\\alpha_i}\\\\! \\\\crk{\\\\!\\\\sup_{z} |\\\\hat{P}_k^i(z;\\\\hat{y}_k^i) - P_k^i(z;\\\\hat{y}_k^i)|}.\\n\\\\end{align}\\nFrom the definition of $e_k^i$, we have\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n    & \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\brk{\\\\|e_k^i \\\\| | \\\\mathcal{H}_k} = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\brk{ \\\\norm{ \\\\frac{d}{\\\\delta_i}\\\\prt{\\\\widehat{C}_k^i(\\\\hat{y}_k^i) - C^i(\\\\hat{y}_k^i)}u_k^i} \\\\bigg| \\\\mathcal{H}_k} \\\\\\\\ \\n    &\\\\le \\\\frac{d}{\\\\delta_i} \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\brk{\\\\norm{ \\\\widehat{C}_k^i(\\\\hat{y}_k^i) - C^i(\\\\hat{y}_k^i)} \\\\bigg| \\\\mathcal{H}_k}  \\\\|u_k^i\\\\| \\\\le  \\\\frac{  dU_i}{\\\\alpha_i\\\\delta_i} \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{s_k^i}}.\\n\\\\end{align*}  \\nThe second inequality uses~\\\\eqref{eq: cvar error} and $\\\\|u_k^i\\\\|=1$. Taking the conditional expectation with respect to $\\\\mathcal F_k$ and applying the tower property gives\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{eq:DKW cvar mismatch F}\\n\\\\mathbb{E}\\\\brk{\\\\mathbb{E}\\\\brk{\\\\|e_k^i\\\\| | \\\\mathcal{H}_k } | \\\\mathcal{F}_k} =  \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\brk{\\\\|e_k^i\\\\| | \\\\mathcal{F}_k}  \\\\le  \\\\frac{ 2dU_i}{\\\\alpha_i\\\\delta_i}  \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2s_k^i}}.\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nSumming~\\\\eqref{eq:DKW cvar mismatch F} over $i\\\\in[m]$ and using~\\\\eqref{eq:sampling requirement} yields\\n\\\\begin{align*}\\n&\\\\sum_{i=1}^m  \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\brk{       \\\\|e_k^i\\\\| \\\\big| \\\\mathcal{F}_k }   \\n   \\\\le  \\\\sum_{i=1}^m\\\\frac{  dU_i}{\\\\alpha_i\\\\delta_i} \\\\sqrt{\\\\frac{2\\\\pi}{s_k^i}} \\\\nonumber \\\\\\\\\\n&  \\\\le    md\\\\sqrt{2 \\\\pi  }\\\\Big\\\\{\\\\max_{i\\\\in \\\\mathcal{V}} \\\\frac{U_i}{\\\\alpha_i\\\\delta_i}\\\\Big\\\\} e_s   .\\n\\\\end{align*}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '180,260p' inputs/Q.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '180,260p' inputs/Q.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{tax_function_eq}\\nt_n(s_n, s_{-n}):=\\\\frac{1}{2}p^\\\\top A^n p+p^\\\\top B^n x+p^\\\\top a^n.\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nHere, $A^n$ is a symmetric matrix and the coefficient matrices and the vector are defined as:\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{tax_matrices_eq}\\n\\tA^n= \\\\left(A_{ml}^n\\\\right)_{m,l\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}, \\\\, B^n= \\\\left(B_{ml}^n\\\\right)_{m,l\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}, \\\\, a^n=(a_m^n)_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}},\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nwhere $A_{ml}^n,\\\\,B_{ml}^n\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{K\\\\times K}$ and $a_m^n\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^K$. The manager's objective is now to systematically determine these parameters so that the desired properties P1-P4 are satisfied.\\n\\\\begin{remark}\\nContrary to existing mechanisms in the literature \\\\cite{kakhbod2012efficient, farhadi2018surrogate, eslami2022incentive}, where the structure and parameters of the payment functions are predetermined, and certain desired properties are investigated given that fully known payment function, this paper systematically constructs the payment function, starting with a parameterized quadratic function \\\\eqref{tax_function_eq} and determining its parameters using LMI optimization method that satisfies the properties P1-P4.\\n\\\\end{remark}\\n\\\\begin{remark}\\nIn mechanism design, the valuation functions of the agents are typically assumed to be unknown to the manager. This also holds in the stochastic setting considered here; thus, evaluating the expectation term is unnecessary when analyzing properties P1-P4. However, for dynamic stability (P5), during the learning process, each agent must work with a sample-based approximation of the valuation functions in order to compute its decision at each iteration.\\n\\\\end{remark}\\n\\\\section{Specification of the payment function}\\\\label{sec4}\\nIn this section, the parameters of $A^n$, $B^n$, and $a^n$ from \\\\eqref{tax_matrices_eq} are determined through an LMI optimization framework  such that the payment functions \\\\eqref{tax_function_eq} satisfy the properties P1-P4 stated in Section \\\\ref{machanism_design_subsec}.\\n\\\\subsection{Formulation of LMI conditions}\\\\label{LMI_subsec}\\nIn this section, LMI conditions are derived on the payment function parameters \\\\eqref{tax_matrices_eq} so that the mechanism satisfies properties P1-P4.\\n\\\\subsubsection{Existence and uniqueness (P1)} The induced game $\\\\mathcal{G}$ under the designed mechanism must admit a unique equilibrium. Proposition \\\\ref{existence_uniqueness_prop} provides LMIs that ensure the existence and uniqueness of NE.\\n\\n\\\\begin{proposition}\\\\label{existence_uniqueness_prop}\\nDefine the matrix $\\\\Psi=(\\\\Psi_{nm})_{n,m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}$ where \\n\\\\[\\\\Psi_{nn}=-\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n    \\\\alpha I_K & B_{nn}^n\\\\\\\\\\n        B_{nn}^{n^\\\\top}& A_{nn}^n\\n\\\\end{pmatrix}, \\n\\\\Psi_{nm}=-\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n    0 & B_{mn}^n\\\\\\\\\\n    B_{nm}^{n^\\\\top} & A_{nm}^n\\n\\\\end{pmatrix},  m\\\\neq n.\\\\] A Nash equilibrium of $\\\\mathcal{G}$ exists and is unique if\\n\\t\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{existence_uniqueness_cond_eq}\\n\\t\\\\textnormal{P1:} \\\\left\\\\{\\n\\t(\\\\romannumeral 1) \\\\Psi_{nn}\\\\preceq 0, \\\\,\\n        (\\\\romannumeral 2) \\\\Psi + \\\\Psi^\\\\top \\\\preceq -\\\\upsilon I_{2KN}\\n\\t\\\\right\\\\},\\n\\t\\\\end{equation}\\n    where $\\\\upsilon>0$ is a scalar design parameter. \\n\\\\end{proposition}\\n\\\\begin{proof}\\nDefine $J:=(J_{nm})_{n,m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}$ where\\n\\\\[\\nJ_{nm}:=\\\\frac{\\\\partial^2 U_n(s_n,s_{-n})}{\\\\partial s_n \\\\partial s_m}.\\n\\\\]\\nBy Assumption \\\\ref{valuation_function_assumption}, we have $\\\\frac{\\\\partial^2 V_n(s_n)}{\\\\partial s_n^2}\\\\preceq -\\\\alpha I_K$, thus\\n\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{unique_eq}\\n     J_{nn}=\\n     \\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n        \\\\frac{\\\\partial^2 V_n(s_n)}{\\\\partial s_n^2} & -B_{nn}^n\\\\\\\\\\n        -B_{nn}^{n^\\\\top}& -A_{nn}^n\\n    \\\\end{pmatrix}\\\\preceq \\n    \\\\Psi_{nn}\\n\\\\end{equation}\\nIf $B_{nn}^n, A_{nn}^n$ are chosen such that\\n$\\\\Psi_{nn}\\\\preceq 0$, i.e., $U_n$ is concave with respect to $s_n$, there exists an equilibrium point for the game $\\\\mathcal{G}$ \\\\cite[Theorem 1]{rosen1965existence}.  \\nFrom \\\\eqref{unique_eq} and the fact that for $m\\\\neq n$, $J_{nm}=\\\\Psi_{nm}$, it follows that $J\\\\preceq \\\\Psi$; therefore, \\n\\\\[\\\\Psi+\\\\Psi^\\\\top\\\\preceq -\\\\upsilon I_{2KN} \\\\Rightarrow J+J^\\\\top\\\\preceq -\\\\upsilon I_{2KN},\\\\]\\nwhich is a sufficient condition for the pseudogradient $\\\\textbf{col}(\\\\nabla_{s_1}U_1(s_1, s_{-1}), \\\\ldots, \\\\nabla_{s_N}U_N(s_N, s_{-N}))$ to be diagonally strictly concave \\\\cite[Theorem 6]{rosen1965existence}, and thus, a sufficient condition for the uniqueness of NE of the game $\\\\mathcal{G}$.\\n\\\\end{proof}\\n\\\\subsubsection{Nash implementation (P2)}\\nFor P2, conditions need to be imposed on the payment rule $t_n$ such that a NE of $\\\\mathcal{G}$ corresponds to the optimal solution of problem \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq}. To achieve this, it is necessary that the KKT point of \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq} is feasible within the KKT conditions of (\\\\ref{utility_maximization_eq}). Theorem \\\\ref{nash_implementation_theorem} determines the LMIs satisfying P2.\\n\\\\begin{theorem}\\\\label{nash_implementation_theorem}\\n\\tThe optimal solution of problem \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq} is a NE of the induced game by \\\\eqref{tax_function_eq}, if the parameters of \\\\eqref{tax_matrices_eq} satisfy\\n\\t\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{nash_implementation_cond_eq}\\n\\t\\\\textnormal{P2:}\\\\left\\\\{\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\t\\t(\\\\romannumeral 1) \\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{A_{nm}^n}=0, (\\\\romannumeral 2) \\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{B_{mn}^n} = \\\\text{diag}(\\\\zeta^{n}),\\\\\\\\\\n\\t\\t(\\\\romannumeral 3) B_{nm}^n=-\\\\text{diag}(\\\\theta^{n}), (\\\\romannumeral 4) a_n^{n}=\\\\text{diag}(\\\\theta^{n})c\\n\\t\\t\\\\end{array}\\\\right\\\\}\\n\\t\\\\end{equation}\\n where $\\\\zeta^{n}, \\\\, \\\\theta^n\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}_+^{K}$.\\n\\\\end{theorem}\\n\\n\\\\begin{proof}\\nLet $\\\\delta_{\\\\mathcal{X}_n}(\\\\cdot)$ denote the indicator function of the local set $\\\\mathcal{X}_n$, i.e.,\\n     \\\\[\\\\delta_{\\\\mathcal{X}_n}(x_n)=\\\\begin{cases}\\n         0,&\\\\text{if} \\\\ x_n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{X}_n,\\\\\\\\\\n         +\\\\infty,&\\\\text{otherwise}.\\n     \\\\end{cases}\\n     \\\\]\\n The KKT conditions of problem \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq} are\\n\\t\\t\\\\begin{subequations}\\\\label{eq10}\\n\\t\\t\\t\\\\begin{align}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '395,440p' inputs/Q.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '395,440p' inputs/Q.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\\\end{proposition}\\n\\\\begin{proof}\\n    Let $s_n'=\\\\textbf{col}(0, \\\\tilde{p})$. We will impose conditions on parameters \\\\eqref{tax_matrices_eq} such that $U_n(s_n', s_{-n}^*)\\\\geq 0$. Using the fact that $V_n(0)=0$, it is sufficient to show that \\n    \\\\begin{equation*}\\n    \\\\begin{aligned}\\n        \\\\begin{split}\\n            t_n(s_n', s_{-n}^*)&=\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\tilde{p}^\\\\top \\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{\\\\sum_{l\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{A_{ml}^n}}\\\\tilde{p} \\\\\\\\&+ \\\\tilde{p}^\\\\top\\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{\\\\sum_{l\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}\\\\backslash n}{B_{ml}^n x_l^o}} + \\\\tilde{p}^\\\\top\\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{a_m^n}\\n        \\\\end{split}\\n    \\\\end{aligned}\\n    \\\\end{equation*}\\n    is non-positive. The quadratic term w.r.t. $\\\\tilde{p}$ has only non-positive values if (\\\\ref{IR_cond_eq}$\\\\romannumeral 1$) is satisfied. Since $\\\\tilde{p}$ and $x_l^o, \\\\, \\\\forall l\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}$, are non-negative vectors, for the other terms to be non-positive, all entries of the vector $\\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}}{a_m^n}$ and the matrices $B_{ml}^n$ must be non-positive, i.e., (\\\\ref{IR_cond_eq}$\\\\romannumeral 2$) and (\\\\ref{IR_cond_eq}$\\\\romannumeral 3$) must hold. Using the definition of NE, we have \\\\begin{equation}U_n(s_n^*, s_{-n}^*)\\\\geq U_n(s_n',s_{-n}^*)\\\\geq 0,\\\\end{equation} and therefore, the mechanism has the individual rationality property.\\n\\\\end{proof}\\n\\n\\\\subsection{A feasible solution to linear matrix inequalities}\\nIn this section, a feasible solution satisfying the LMI conditions of Propositions \\\\ref{existence_uniqueness_prop}-\\\\ref{IR_prop} and Theorems \\\\ref{nash_implementation_theorem}-\\\\ref{theorem3} from Section \\\\ref{LMI_subsec} is found; hence, a quadratic payment function is designed that results in the desired properties P1-P4. The following proposition introduces such a payment function.\\n\\\\begin{proposition}\\\\label{prop2}\\n\\tThe payment function in \\\\eqref{eq40} satisfies the LMIs \\\\eqref{existence_uniqueness_cond_eq}, \\\\eqref{nash_implementation_cond_eq}, \\\\eqref{eq19}, and \\\\eqref{IR_cond_eq}, and hence results in a mechanism satisfying properties P1\u2013P4.\\n\\t\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{eq40}\\n\\t\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\t\\\\begin{split}\\n\\tt_n(&s_n, s_{-n})=\\\\alpha\\\\bigg[\\\\frac{1}{2}p_n^\\\\top(p_n-\\\\frac{2}{N-1}\\\\bar{p}_{-n})\\\\\\\\&-\\\\frac{1}{N-1}p_n^\\\\top(x_n+\\\\bar{x}_{-n}-c)+\\\\frac{N}{(N-1)^2}\\\\bar{p}_{-n}^\\\\top(x_n-\\\\frac{c}{N})\\\\\\\\&+\\\\frac{1}{2(N-1)}\\\\bar{\\\\sigma}_{-n}^p-\\\\frac{1}{(N-1)^2}(\\\\bar{\\\\sigma}_{-n}^{px}-\\\\bar{p}_{-n}^\\\\top\\\\frac{c}{N})\\\\bigg]\\\\\\\\\\n\\t\\\\end{split}\\n\\t\\\\end{aligned}\\n\\t\\\\end{equation}\\n\\twhere \\n    \\\\begin{align*}\\n        &\\\\bar{p}_{-n}=\\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}\\\\backslash n}{p_m}, \\\\quad \\\\bar{x}_{-n}=\\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}\\\\backslash n}{x_m},\\\\\\\\\\n        &\\\\bar{\\\\sigma}_{-n}^p=\\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}\\\\backslash n}{p_m^\\\\top p_m},\\\\quad \\\\bar{\\\\sigma}_{-n}^{px}=\\\\sum_{m\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}\\\\backslash n}{p_m^\\\\top x_m}.\\n    \\\\end{align*}\\n\\\\end{proposition}\\n\\\\begin{proof}\\n\\tThe proof can be straightforwardly deduced by directly applying conditions \\\\eqref{existence_uniqueness_cond_eq}, (\\\\ref{nash_implementation_cond_eq}), (\\\\ref{eq19}), and (\\\\ref{IR_cond_eq}) to (\\\\ref{eq40}).\\n\\\\end{proof}\\nIn (\\\\ref{eq40}), the quadratic term with respect to $s_n$ penalizes deviation from uniform pricing. The second term shows the payment corresponding to the coupling constraint, meaning if agents violate the limitations of resources, they are fined, and if they use less than the maximum amount of the resources, they get paid. The expression $\\\\bar{p}_{-n}^\\\\top(x_n-\\\\frac{c}{N})$  depicts the payment of agent $n$ based on the aggregate proposed price of others. The additional components, which are quadratic terms with respect to $s_{-n}$, are needed to satisfy the LMI conditions. At the equilibrium point, this payment function results in a uniform price which is equal to $\\\\lambda^o/\\\\alpha$, the Lagrange multiplier associated with each constraint in \\\\eqref{manager_problem_eq}, and also yields feasible demands that satisfy the coupling constraints. \\n\\\\section{Stochastic Dynamic Stability}\\nIn this section, the dynamic stability (P5) of the induced game is investigated and a decentralized variable sample-size proximal best-response (VS-PBR) \\\\cite{lei2022distributed} algorithm with Krasnoselskij iteration is proposed, through which the agents learn their NE strategy with partial knowledge about their rivals' decisions. \\n\\\\par\\nBy eliminating terms in \\\\eqref{eq40} that do not depend on the strategy of user $n\\\\in\\\\mathcal{N}$, we can define \\\\[\\\\hat{t}_n(s_n, \\\\bar{s}_{-n}):=\\\\alpha [s_n^\\\\top Ms_n + (\\\\Delta \\\\bar{s}_{-n}+d)^\\\\top s_n]\\\\] where $\\\\bar{s}_{-n}=\\\\sum_{m\\\\in \\\\mathcal{N}\\\\backslash n}{s_m}$, $d=\\\\textbf{col}(0, \\\\frac{c}{N-1})$, and \\n\\\\[\\n            M=-\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n                0 & 0\\\\\\\\\\n                \\\\frac{1}{N-1}I_{K} & \\\\frac{1}{2}I_{K}\\n            \\\\end{pmatrix},  \\\\Delta = \\\\frac{-1}{N-1}\\\\begin{pmatrix}\\n                0 & I_{K}\\\\\\\\\\n                \\\\frac{-N}{(N-1)}I_{K} & I_{K}\\n            \\\\end{pmatrix}.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'if [ -n \\\"$HERDR_PANE_ID\\\" ]; then herdr pane rename \\\"$HERDR_PANE_ID\\\" \\\"Q\u2013P CVaR mechanism check\\\"; else print -r -- '\\\"'HERDR_PANE_ID unset'; fi\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'if [ -n \\\"$HERDR_PANE_ID\\\" ]; then herdr pane rename \\\"$HERDR_PANE_ID\\\" \\\"Q\u2013P CVaR mechanism check\\\"; else print -r -- '\\\"'HERDR_PANE_ID unset'; fi\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"HERDR_PANE_ID unset\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"My direct expansion agrees with emmy\u2019s correction: the \\\\(N/(N-1)^2\\\\) term in Q\u2019s tax contributes \\\\(N/(N-1)\\\\) at a common price. I\u2019m adding the full expansion to the note so the general-\\\\(N\\\\) coefficient is auditable, then I\u2019ll run the required style checks.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: style-gates\\ndescription: >-\\n  Run and enforce lightweight style gates for Markdown, LaTeX, and PDF artifacts before Codex\\n  shares, commits, pushes, or produces .md, .tex, or .pdf files. Use when writing, editing,\\n  reviewing, generating, or pushing reports, papers, slides, documentation, markdown notes,\\n  TeX sources, or PDFs that should pass basic style and integrity checks.\\n---\\n\\n# Style Gates\\n\\n## Core Rule\\n\\nBefore finalizing, sharing, committing, or pushing any `.md`, `.tex`, or `.pdf` artifact,\\nidentify the affected files and run the gate. Fix failures before delivery. If a failure is\\nintentionally left unresolved, say exactly which gate failed and why.\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nFrom the repository root, run:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed\\n```\\n\\nBefore commit or push, prefer:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --staged\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed --base origin/main\\n```\\n\\nFor a specific artifact:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py report.md paper.tex slides.pdf\\n```\\n\\nUse `--strict-warnings` when the recipient wants warnings to block delivery.\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n1. Determine scope:\\n   - For generated files, pass the exact output paths.\\n   - For a commit, use `--staged`.\\n   - For a branch or push, use `--changed --base origin/main` or the relevant upstream base.\\n   - For an initial audit, use `--all`.\\n2. Run the gate script.\\n3. Fix all errors. Treat warnings as review prompts unless `--strict-warnings` is required.\\n4. For LaTeX and PDFs, also use project-native validation when available:\\n   - Compile `.tex` with the repo's existing command or `latexmk`/`pdflatex`.\\n   - Open or inspect generated PDFs enough to confirm they are not blank, corrupt, or stale.\\n5. Re-run the gate after edits.\\n\\n## Human Review Gates\\n\\nThe script catches mechanical issues; still review these before sharing.\\n\\nMarkdown:\\n- Clear title and section hierarchy.\\n- No unresolved placeholders, merge markers, stale TODOs, or fake links.\\n- Tables, lists, math, and fenced code render plausibly.\\n- Claims with external facts have sources or are clearly marked as assumptions.\\n\\nLaTeX:\\n- The file compiles, or the reason it was not compiled is reported.\\n- No unresolved citations/references in the build log when a PDF is produced.\\n- No obvious placeholder text, broken environments, or empty `\\\\ref{}` / `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n- Generated PDFs correspond to the current `.tex`, not an older build artifact.\\n\\nPDF:\\n- File opens, has at least one page, and is not zero-byte/corrupt.\\n- Expected text is visible/extractable unless the PDF is intentionally image-only.\\n- No accidental blank pages, cropped content, or stale draft/watermark text.\\n\\n## Integration\\n\\nFor repo hooks, CI, and copy/paste setup, read `references/repo-integration.md`.\\nTemplates live in `assets/`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I\u2019m using the style-gates skill for the revised Markdown note. The algebra also yields a slightly stronger identity: with equal prices, the rebate reduces the transfer to \\\\(\\\\alpha p^\\\\top(x_n-c/N)\\\\) even before imposing complementarity.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/ada/pair_note.md\",\"kind\":\"update\"}],\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q7P1/ada/pair_note.md\",\"kind\":\"update\"}],\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff -- ada/pair_note.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff -- ada/pair_note.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom fractions import Fraction as F\\nfor N in range(2, 9):\\n    c=F(N)\\n    x=[F(i) for i in range(1,N+1)]\\n    x[-1]=c-sum(x[:-1])\\n    p=F(3,2)\\n    n=0\\n    barp=(N-1)*p\\n    barx=sum(x[1:])\\n    sigp=(N-1)*p*p\\n    sigpx=p*barx\\n    old=F(1,2)*p*(p-F(2,N-1)*barp)-F(1,N-1)*p*(sum(x)-c)+F(N,(N-1)**2)*barp*(x[n]-c/N)+F(1,2*(N-1))*sigp-F(1,(N-1)**2)*(sigpx-barp*c/N)\\n    kappa=F(N*N-N+1,(N-1)**2)\\n    rebate=(kappa-1)*(barp/(N-1))*(barx-F(N-1,N)*c)\\n    target=p*(x[n]-c/N)\\n    assert old+rebate==target,(N,old,rebate,target)\\n    print(N,old,rebate,target)\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom fractions import Fraction as F\\nfor N in range(2, 9):\\n    c=F(N)\\n    x=[F(i) for i in range(1,N+1)]\\n    x[-1]=c-sum(x[:-1])\\n    p=F(3,2)\\n    n=0\\n    barp=(N-1)*p\\n    barx=sum(x[1:])\\n    sigp=(N-1)*p*p\\n    sigpx=p*barx\\n    old=F(1,2)*p*(p-F(2,N-1)*barp)-F(1,N-1)*p*(sum(x)-c)+F(N,(N-1)**2)*barp*(x[n]-c/N)+F(1,2*(N-1))*sigp-F(1,(N-1)**2)*(sigpx-barp*c/N)\\n    kappa=F(N*N-N+1,(N-1)**2)\\n    rebate=(kappa-1)*(barp/(N-1))*(barx-F(N-1,N)*c)\\n    target=p*(x[n]-c/N)\\n    assert old+rebate==target,(N,old,rebate,target)\\n    print(N,old,rebate,target)\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"2 0 0 0\\n3 0 0 0\\n4 0 0 0\\n5 0 0 0\\n6 0 0 0\\n7 0 0 0\\n8 0 0 0\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'python3 /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py ada/pair_note.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"i