\\) is computable and statistical calibration adds no value. A new varied-schedule, hidden-value deployment is necessary; then the \\\\\\\\(4m\\\\\\\\varepsilon\\\\\\\\) bound may be too loose (e.g. \\\\\\\\(m=11,\\\\\\\\varepsilon=0.25\\\\\\\\) gives a margin of \\\\\\\\(11\\\\\\\\), exceeding Q\u2019s total optimal surplus \\\\\\\\(7.5\\\\\\\\)).' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind finding --text 'Cross-check of Ada #13 against Q \u00a7Market Environment/Trading Mechanism and P Theorem 1: the Q-specific example is valid. The forced pair has surplus \\\\\\\\(1.00-0.75=0.25\\\\\\\\), best continuation adds \\\\\\\\(3.25-3.00=0.25\\\\\\\\), whereas the original optimum is \\\\\\\\(3.25-0.75=2.50\\\\\\\\), so \\\\\\\\(D_e=2.00\\\\\\\\). The bound \\\\\\\\(D_e(x)\\\\\\\\le D_e(\\\\\\\\widehat x)+4m\\\\\\\\varepsilon\\\\\\\\) follows because each matching with at most \\\\\\\\(m\\\\\\\\) trades changes welfare by at most \\\\\\\\(2m\\\\\\\\varepsilon\\\\\\\\). Crucially, this certifies attainable continuation surplus, not realized welfare, as P itself limits its gate claim. Q uses a single fixed, evaluator-known reservation schedule, so here exact \\\\\\\\(D_e\\\\\\\\) is computable and statistical calibration adds no value. A new varied-schedule, hidden-value deployment is necessary; then the \\\\\\\\(4m\\\\\\\\varepsilon\\\\\\\\) bound may be too loose (e.g. \\\\\\\\(m=11,\\\\\\\\varepsilon=0.25\\\\\\\\) gives a margin of \\\\\\\\(11\\\\\\\\), exceeding Q\u2019s total optimal surplus \\\\\\\\(7.5\\\\\\\\)).' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"15\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy read --since 13'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy read --since 13'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#14 [ada/finding] Cross-check of Emmy #11 yields a stronger result from Q Table 1 alone. Since \\\\(W_{\\\\max}(N)=2.75N-0.25N^2\\\\), \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[W^*-W_{\\\\max}(N)]=7.5-W_{\\\\max}(\\\\bar N)+0.25\\\\operatorname{Var}(N)\\\\). Integer-valued \\\\(N\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\bar N=2.2\\\\) has \\\\(\\\\operatorname{Var}(N)\\\\ge0.2(0.8)=0.16\\\\). For GPT Large round 4, \\\\(W_{\\\\max}(2.2)=4.84\\\\), so mean quantity loss is at least \\\\(7.5-4.84+0.04=2.70\\\\). Q reports mean efficiency \\\\(0.36\\\\), hence total mean welfare loss about \\\\(7.5(1-0.36)=4.80\\\\): at least \\\\(56.25\\\\%\\\\) of that loss is attributable to trade count, at most \\\\(2.10\\\\) surplus units to trader selection. Rounding of the table makes percentages approximate. This partially supports Q's stalling mechanism without claiming it explains all inefficiency. The same integer-variance refinement can be computed for all rows. P's interface warning motivates distinguishing the two components, but this bound is specific to Q's value schedule.\\n#15 [emmy/finding] Cross-check of Ada #13 against Q \u00a7Market Environment/Trading Mechanism and P Theorem 1: the Q-specific example is valid. The forced pair has surplus \\\\(1.00-0.75=0.25\\\\), best continuation adds \\\\(3.25-3.00=0.25\\\\), whereas the original optimum is \\\\(3.25-0.75=2.50\\\\), so \\\\(D_e=2.00\\\\). The bound \\\\(D_e(x)\\\\le D_e(\\\\widehat x)+4m\\\\varepsilon\\\\) follows because each matching with at most \\\\(m\\\\) trades changes welfare by at most \\\\(2m\\\\varepsilon\\\\). Crucially, this certifies attainable continuation surplus, not realized welfare, as P itself limits its gate claim. Q uses a single fixed, evaluator-known reservation schedule, so here exact \\\\(D_e\\\\) is computable and statistical calibration adds no value. A new varied-schedule, hidden-value deployment is necessary; then the \\\\(4m\\\\varepsilon\\\\) bound may be too loose (e.g. \\\\(m=11,\\\\varepsilon=0.25\\\\) gives a margin of \\\\(11\\\\), exceeding Q\u2019s total optimal surplus \\\\(7.5\\\\)).\\nlatest substantive entry: 15\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nrows=[('Gemini',1,5.4,.83),('Gemini',2,4.6,.70),('Gemini',3,3.9,.66),('Gemini',4,3.3,.58),('Gemini',5,4.1,.72),('GPT Large',1,2.6,.47),('GPT Large',2,3.8,.59),('GPT Large',3,3.0,.56),('GPT Large',4,2.2,.36),('GPT Large',5,3.5,.67),('GPT Small',1,5.5,.79),('GPT Small',2,5.7,.86),('GPT Small',3,5.7,.89),('GPT Small',4,5.8,.89),('GPT Small',5,6.2,.91)]\\nfor model,r,mu,eff in rows:\\n    frac=mu%1\\n    qty=7.5-(2.75*mu-.25*mu*mu)+.25*frac*(1-frac)\\n    loss=7.5*(1-eff)\\n    print(model,r, 'qty_min',round(qty,3),'loss',round(loss,3),'frac_min',round(qty/loss,3))\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nrows=[('Gemini',1,5.4,.83),('Gemini',2,4.6,.70),('Gemini',3,3.9,.66),('Gemini',4,3.3,.58),('Gemini',5,4.1,.72),('GPT Large',1,2.6,.47),('GPT Large',2,3.8,.59),('GPT Large',3,3.0,.56),('GPT Large',4,2.2,.36),('GPT Large',5,3.5,.67),('GPT Small',1,5.5,.79),('GPT Small',2,5.7,.86),('GPT Small',3,5.7,.89),('GPT Small',4,5.8,.89),('GPT Small',5,6.2,.91)]\\nfor model,r,mu,eff in rows:\\n    frac=mu%1\\n    qty=7.5-(2.75*mu-.25*mu*mu)+.25*frac*(1-frac)\\n    loss=7.5*(1-eff)\\n    print(model,r, 'qty_min',round(qty,3),'loss',round(loss,3),'frac_min',round(qty/loss,3))\\nPY\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Gemini 1 qty_min -0.0 loss 1.275 frac_min -0.0\\nGemini 2 qty_min 0.2 loss 2.25 frac_min 0.089\\nGemini 3 qty_min 0.6 loss 2.55 frac_min 0.235\\nGemini 4 qty_min 1.2 loss 3.15 frac_min 0.381\\nGemini 5 qty_min 0.45 loss 2.1 frac_min 0.214\\nGPT Large 1 qty_min 2.1 loss 3.975 frac_min 0.528\\nGPT Large 2 qty_min 0.7 loss 3.075 frac_min 0.228\\nGPT Large 3 qty_min 1.5 loss 3.3 frac_min 0.455\\nGPT Large 4 qty_min 2.7 loss 4.8 frac_min 0.562\\nGPT Large 5 qty_min 1.0 loss 2.475 frac_min 0.404\\nGPT Small 1 qty_min 0.0 loss 1.575 frac_min 0.0\\nGPT Small 2 qty_min -0.0 loss 1.05 frac_min -0.0\\nGPT Small 3 qty_min -0.0 loss 0.825 frac_min -0.0\\nGPT Small 4 qty_min 0.0 loss 0.825 frac_min 0.0\\nGPT Small 5 qty_min 0.1 loss 0.675 frac_min 0.148\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Ada found a useful correction to my earlier assessment: Q\u2019s summary table cannot give the exact welfare split, but it does give a lower bound on loss from too few trades. I\u2019ve checked the arithmetic and will record that correction, then assess whether the proposed crossing certificate would be informative in Q\u2019s setting.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind correction --text 'Ada #14 corrects my #7/#11 statement that Q Table 1 cannot quantify either component. Exact components need run records, but the table bounds quantity loss below. With \\\\\\\\(f(N)=W_{\\\\\\\\max}(N)=2.75N-0.25N\\\"'^2'\\\"\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mathbb E[7.5-f(N)]=7.5-f(\\\\\\\\mu)+0.25\\\\\\\\operatorname{Var}(N)\\\\\\\\). For integer counts and \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mu=2.2\\\\\\\\), variance is at least \\\\\\\\(0.16\\\\\\\\). Thus GPT Large round 4 has mean quantity loss at least \\\\\\\\(2.70\\\\\\\\), versus reported total loss about \\\\\\\\(4.80\\\\\\\\), so at least roughly \\\\\\\\(56\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\) of the deficit is necessarily due to trade count. This is a substantive table-level check of Q\u2019s stalling explanation; selection can still explain up to \\\\\\\\(2.10\\\\\\\\) units, and table rounding makes the percentage approximate. The same bound gives at least \\\\\\\\(2.10\\\\\\\\) of \\\\\\\\(3.975\\\\\\\\) total loss in GPT Large round 1, about \\\\\\\\(53\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\).' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind correction --text 'Ada #14 corrects my #7/#11 statement that Q Table 1 cannot quantify either component. Exact components need run records, but the table bounds quantity loss below. With \\\\\\\\(f(N)=W_{\\\\\\\\max}(N)=2.75N-0.25N\\\"'^2'\\\"\\\\\\\\), \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mathbb E[7.5-f(N)]=7.5-f(\\\\\\\\mu)+0.25\\\\\\\\operatorname{Var}(N)\\\\\\\\). For integer counts and \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mu=2.2\\\\\\\\), variance is at least \\\\\\\\(0.16\\\\\\\\). Thus GPT Large round 4 has mean quantity loss at least \\\\\\\\(2.70\\\\\\\\), versus reported total loss about \\\\\\\\(4.80\\\\\\\\), so at least roughly \\\\\\\\(56\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\) of the deficit is necessarily due to trade count. This is a substantive table-level check of Q\u2019s stalling explanation; selection can still explain up to \\\\\\\\(2.10\\\\\\\\) units, and table rounding makes the percentage approximate. The same bound gives at least \\\\\\\\(2.10\\\\\\\\) of \\\\\\\\(3.975\\\\\\\\) total loss in GPT Large round 1, about \\\\\\\\(53\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\).' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"16\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind idea --text 'Possible sharper version of Ada #13\u2019s selective margin: calibrate the *opportunity-cost error* directly rather than first calibrating \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\lVert x-\\\\\\\\widehat x\\\\\\\\rVert_\\\\\\\\infty\\\\\\\\) and multiplying by \\\\\\\\(4m\\\\\\\\). For each exchangeable calibration market, set \\\\\\\\(R_i=\\\\\\\\max_{(S,e)\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal C_i}|D_e(x_i\\\"'^S)-D_e('\\\"\\\\\\\\widehat x_i\\\"'^S)|'\\\"\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mathcal C_i\\\\\\\\) is a prespecified set of candidate remaining states and edges. The conformal order statistic \\\\\\\\(q\\\\\\\\) then permits the label \\\\\\\\(D_e(\\\\\\\\widehat x\\\"'^S)+q'\\\"\\\\\\\\le\\\\\\\\tau\\\\\\\\) for every candidate in a new covered market, by P\u2019s single-episode rank argument. If \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mathcal C_i\\\\\\\\) includes all subsets and edges, selection can be arbitrary after calibration; this may be computationally costly and conservative. If it includes only states reached under a fixed policy, calibration and deployment must use that same frozen policy; an intervention needs its own episodes. This targeted score could be less vacuous than \\\\\\\\(4m\\\\\\\\varepsilon\\\\\\\\), but neither paper supplies the needed hidden-value predictor, varied schedules, or calibration data, and no nonvacuity claim follows.' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind idea --text 'Possible sharper version of Ada #13\u2019s selective margin: calibrate the *opportunity-cost error* directly rather than first calibrating \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\lVert x-\\\\\\\\widehat x\\\\\\\\rVert_\\\\\\\\infty\\\\\\\\) and multiplying by \\\\\\\\(4m\\\\\\\\). For each exchangeable calibration market, set \\\\\\\\(R_i=\\\\\\\\max_{(S,e)\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\mathcal C_i}|D_e(x_i\\\"'^S)-D_e('\\\"\\\\\\\\widehat x_i\\\"'^S)|'\\\"\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mathcal C_i\\\\\\\\) is a prespecified set of candidate remaining states and edges. The conformal order statistic \\\\\\\\(q\\\\\\\\) then permits the label \\\\\\\\(D_e(\\\\\\\\widehat x\\\"'^S)+q'\\\"\\\\\\\\le\\\\\\\\tau\\\\\\\\) for every candidate in a new covered market, by P\u2019s single-episode rank argument. If \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\mathcal C_i\\\\\\\\) includes all subsets and edges, selection can be arbitrary after calibration; this may be computationally costly and conservative. If it includes only states reached under a fixed policy, calibration and deployment must use that same frozen policy; an intervention needs its own episodes. This targeted score could be less vacuous than \\\\\\\\(4m\\\\\\\\varepsilon\\\\\\\\), but neither paper supplies the needed hidden-value predictor, varied schedules, or calibration data, and no nonvacuity claim follows.' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"17\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind correction --text 'Qualification to my #17: a policy that changes crossings based on the calibrated threshold \\\\\\\\(q\\\\\\\\) is not fixed before calibration, so scores from trajectories generated by a different provisional threshold need not be exchangeable with deployment trajectories. The clean simultaneous construction scores all feasible remaining states and candidate edges from each episode\u2019s full ground-truth vector, independent of the eventual policy. A trajectory-only score is valid only when the trajectory-generating policy is genuinely frozen independently of that score calibration (or after a separate pilot stage fixes the policy and the final calibration does not change it). This dependence is material for a proposed stall-breaking intervention.' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy add --kind correction --text 'Qualification to my #17: a policy that changes crossings based on the calibrated threshold \\\\\\\\(q\\\\\\\\) is not fixed before calibration, so scores from trajectories generated by a different provisional threshold need not be exchangeable with deployment trajectories. The clean simultaneous construction scores all feasible remaining states and candidate edges from each episode\u2019s full ground-truth vector, independent of the eventual policy. A trajectory-only score is valid only when the trajectory-generating policy is genuinely frozen independently of that score calibration (or after a separate pilot stage fixes the policy and the final calibration does not change it). This dependence is material for a proposed stall-breaking intervention.' \\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"19\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy read --since 15'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy read --since 15'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#16 [emmy/correction] Ada #14 corrects my #7/#11 statement that Q Table 1 cannot quantify either component. Exact components need run records, but the table bounds quantity loss below. With \\\\(f(N)=W_{\\\\max}(N)=2.75N-0.25N^2\\\\), \\\\(\\\\mathbb E[7.5-f(N)]=7.5-f(\\\\mu)+0.25\\\\operatorname{Var}(N)\\\\). For integer counts and \\\\(\\\\mu=2.2\\\\), variance is at least \\\\(0.16\\\\). Thus GPT Large round 4 has mean quantity loss at least \\\\(2.70\\\\), versus reported total loss about \\\\(4.80\\\\), so at least roughly \\\\(56\\\\%\\\\) of the deficit is necessarily due to trade count. This is a substantive table-level check of Q\u2019s stalling explanation; selection can still explain up to \\\\(2.10\\\\) units, and table rounding makes the percentage approximate. The same bound gives at least \\\\(2.10\\\\) of \\\\(3.975\\\\) total loss in GPT Large round 1, about \\\\(53\\\\%\\\\).\\n#17 [emmy/idea] Possible sharper version of Ada #13\u2019s selective margin: calibrate the *opportunity-cost error* directly rather than first calibrating \\\\(\\\\lVert x-\\\\widehat x\\\\rVert_\\\\infty\\\\) and multiplying by \\\\(4m\\\\). For each exchangeable calibration market, set \\\\(R_i=\\\\max_{(S,e)\\\\in\\\\mathcal C_i}|D_e(x_i^S)-D_e(\\\\widehat x_i^S)|\\\\), where \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_i\\\\) is a prespecified set of candidate remaining states and edges. The conformal order statistic \\\\(q\\\\) then permits the label \\\\(D_e(\\\\widehat x^S)+q\\\\le\\\\tau\\\\) for every candidate in a new covered market, by P\u2019s single-episode rank argument. If \\\\(\\\\mathcal C_i\\\\) includes all subsets and edges, selection can be arbitrary after calibration; this may be computationally costly and conservative. If it includes only states reached under a fixed policy, calibration and deployment must use that same frozen policy; an intervention needs its own episodes. This targeted score could be less vacuous than \\\\(4m\\\\varepsilon\\\\), but neither paper supplies the needed hidden-value predictor, varied schedules, or calibration data, and no nonvacuity claim follows.\\n#18 [ada/intention] Current synthesis and priority: ada/aggregate_welfare.md gives the strongest finding available from the assigned texts: in Q's GPT Large condition, the published means force at least about \\\\(45\\\\%\\\\) of the five-round aggregate welfare gap to come from trade count, with round 4 at least about \\\\(56\\\\%\\\\); the rest may reflect selection. P's episode-level calibration supplies the proper route to a future-run tail statement, but ten Q runs cannot support a finite \\\\(95\\\\%\\\\) split-conformal bound. A publishable next study would prespecify and randomize a crossing intervention, measure count and selection loss per run, and calibrate each frozen policy separately. My opportunity-cost margin in ada/market_margin.md is a distinct conditional theorem, but it requires hidden-value predictions absent from Q, and Emmy #15 shows its \\\\(4m\\\\varepsilon\\\\) term could swamp the total \\\\(7.5\\\\) surplus. I therefore treat it as a secondary lead, not an established improvement. The pair supports a precise research question and an analytical bound, not an empirical intervention result.\\n#19 [emmy/correction] Qualification to my #17: a policy that changes crossings based on the calibrated threshold \\\\(q\\\\) is not fixed before calibration, so scores from trajectories generated by a different provisional threshold need not be exchangeable with deployment trajectories. The clean simultaneous construction scores all feasible remaining states and candidate edges from each episode\u2019s full ground-truth vector, independent of the eventual policy. A trajectory-only score is valid only when the trajectory-generating policy is genuinely frozen independently of that score calibration (or after a separate pilot stage fixes the policy and the final calibration does not change it). This dependence is material for a proposed stall-breaking intervention.\\nlatest substantive entry: 19\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat ada/aggregate_welfare.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat ada/aggregate_welfare.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# What Q's mean table identifies about welfare loss\\n\\n## Source claims\\n\\nQ, Section \\\"Market Environment,\\\" specifies eleven buyer values and eleven\\nseller costs, each running from \\\\(0.75\\\\) to \\\\(3.25\\\\) in steps of \\\\(0.25\\\\).\\nQ, Section \\\"Market-Wide Outcomes,\\\" reports the mean trade count and mean\\nallocative efficiency from ten simulations for each model and round.\\nIt attributes the weak GPT Large results partly to reluctance to cross\\nthe spread. P, Sections \\\"Calibrated selective certificate\\\" and\\n\\\"Limitations,\\\" makes a useful methodological distinction: a guarantee\\nfor an early gate does not itself certify the downstream outcome. The\\nmarket analogue is to measure the *realized* surplus gap and distinguish\\nits count and selection components.\\n\\n## Exact decomposition\\n\\nWith exactly \\\\(k\\\\) trades, maximum surplus selects the \\\\(k\\\\) highest\\nbuyer values and \\\\(k\\\\) lowest seller costs. For \\\\(0\\\\le k\\\\le11\\\\),\\n\\n\\\\[\\nW_{\\\\max}(k)\\n=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{k}\\\\left(2.5-0.5(j-1)\\\\right)\\n=2.75k-0.25k^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe unconstrained maximum is \\\\(W^*=7.5\\\\), attained by either five or\\nsix optimally selected trades. For realized count \\\\(N\\\\) and realized\\nsurplus \\\\(W\\\\), define\\n\\n\\\\[\\nQ_{\\\\mathrm{loss}}=W^*-W_{\\\\max}(N),\\\\qquad\\nS_{\\\\mathrm{loss}}=W_{\\\\max}(N)-W.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nBoth are nonnegative and\\n\\\\(W^*-W=Q_{\\\\mathrm{loss}}+S_{\\\\mathrm{loss}}\\\\). The first is loss\\nforced by the number of trades. The second is loss due to the identities\\nof the traders who transacted. Transaction prices cancel from \\\\(W\\\\).\\nThis remains true even though Q's exchange permits a transaction that\\nviolates an agent's reservation price.\\n\\n## A lower bound from the published means\\n\\nLet \\\\(\\\\mu=\\\\mathbb E N\\\\), \\\\(a=\\\\lfloor\\\\mu\\\\rfloor\\\\), and \\\\(f=\\\\mu-a\\\\).\\nBecause \\\\(W_{\\\\max}(k)\\\\) is concave on integer \\\\(k\\\\), its greatest possible\\nmean at this \\\\(\\\\mu\\\\) occurs when \\\\(N\\\\) takes only \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(a+1\\\\):\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E W_{\\\\max}(N)\\n\\\\le (1-f)W_{\\\\max}(a)+fW_{\\\\max}(a+1).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nEquivalently,\\n\\\\(\\\\mathbb E W_{\\\\max}(N)\\n=W_{\\\\max}(\\\\mu)-0.25\\\\operatorname{Var}(N)\\\\), and integer-valued\\n\\\\(N\\\\) has \\\\(\\\\operatorname{Var}(N)\\\\ge f(1-f)\\\\). Therefore\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathbb E Q_{\\\\mathrm{loss}}\\n\\\\ge 7.5-W_{\\\\max}(\\\\mu)+0.25f(1-f).\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFor GPT Large in round 4, Q reports \\\\(\\\\mu=2.2\\\\) and mean efficiency\\n\\\\(0.36\\\\). The best possible mean fixed-count surplus is\\n\\\\(0.8W_{\\\\max}(2)+0.2W_{\\\\max}(3)=0.8(4.5)+0.2(6)=4.8\\\\).\\nHence mean quantity loss is at least \\\\(7.5-4.8=2.7\\\\) surplus\\nunits. Mean total welfare loss is approximately\\n\\\\(7.5(1-0.36)=4.8\\\\), so at least about \\\\(56\\\\%\\\\) of this gap\\ncomes from count alone. The remainder, at most \\\\(2.1\\\\) units,\\ncould be trader selection loss. Q's rounded efficiency makes the\\npercentage approximate.\\n\\nApplying the same calculation to the five GPT Large rows gives:\\n\\n| Round | Mean trades | Minimum mean count loss | Mean total loss | Minimum count share |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 2.6 | 2.10 | 3.975 | 53% |\\n| 2 | 3.8 | 0.70 | 3.075 | 23% |\\n| 3 | 3.0 | 1.50 | 3.300 | 45% |\\n| 4 | 2.2 | 2.70 | 4.800 | 56% |\\n| 5 | 3.5 | 1.00 | 2.475 | 40% |\\n\\nAcross these five rows, at least about \\\\(45\\\\%\\\\) of the aggregate\\nreported welfare deficit is forced by trade count. This supports\\nQ's stalling explanation in a limited quantitative sense. It does not\\nshow that incremental bidding *caused* the low counts, and it leaves\\nthe actual selection loss unidentified. The run-level records are\\nneeded to compute both components exactly.\\n\\n## The cross-paper test\\n\\nP's episode-level rank construction could be applied to a complete\\nfive-round market run, using a score such as the maximum total welfare\\ngap, quantity loss, or selection loss across its rounds. This would\\ngive a marginal simultaneous bound for a new run of a *frozen* model,\\nprompt, and mechanism, provided calibration runs are exchangeable.\\nThe policy and any intervention must be calibrated separately. With\\nonly ten calibration runs, a finite \\\\(95\\\\%\\\\) split-conformal order\\nstatistic is unavailable: \\\\(k=\\\\lceil11(0.95)\\\\rceil=11>10\\\\).\\n\\nA publishable test would use run-level data and a prespecified\\nrandomized crossing intervention to ask whether the intervention\\nreduces quantity loss without increasing selection loss or the lower\\ntail of total welfare loss. The table bound is an analytical finding;\\nneither source paper supplies the required intervention result.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The aggregate bound checks out: Q\u2019s reported means force at least about 45% of GPT Large\u2019s five-round welfare gap to come from trade count. I\u2019ll update my derivation note, which still says the table cannot assign any share of the loss. I\u2019ll use the style-gates skill for that Markdown edit.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/v/.codex/skills/style-gates/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: style-gates\\ndescription: >-\\n  Run and enforce lightweight style gates for Markdown, LaTeX, and PDF artifacts before Codex\\n  shares, commits, pushes, or produces .md, .tex, or .pdf files. Use when writing, editing,\\n  reviewing, generating, or pushing reports, papers, slides, documentation, markdown notes,\\n  TeX sources, or PDFs that should pass basic style and integrity checks.\\n---\\n\\n# Style Gates\\n\\n## Core Rule\\n\\nBefore finalizing, sharing, committing, or pushing any `.md`, `.tex`, or `.pdf` artifact,\\nidentify the affected files and run the gate. Fix failures before delivery. If a failure is\\nintentionally left unresolved, say exactly which gate failed and why.\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nFrom the repository root, run:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed\\n```\\n\\nBefore commit or push, prefer:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --staged\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py --changed --base origin/main\\n```\\n\\nFor a specific artifact:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 path/to/style-gates/scripts/style_gate.py report.md paper.tex slides.pdf\\n```\\n\\nUse `--strict-warnings` when the recipient wants warnings to block delivery.\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\n1. Determine scope:\\n   - For generated files, pass the exact output paths.\\n   - For a commit, use `--staged`.\\n   - For a branch or push, use `--changed --base origin/main` or the relevant upstream base.\\n   - For an initial audit, use `--all`.\\n2. Run the gate script.\\n3. Fix all errors. Treat warnings as review prompts unless `--strict-warnings` is required.\\n4. For LaTeX and PDFs, also use project-native validation when available:\\n   - Compile `.tex` with the repo's existing command or `latexmk`/`pdflatex`.\\n   - Open or inspect generated PDFs enough to confirm they are not blank, corrupt, or stale.\\n5. Re-run the gate after edits.\\n\\n## Human Review Gates\\n\\nThe script catches mechanical issues; still review these before sharing.\\n\\nMarkdown:\\n- Clear title and section hierarchy.\\n- No unresolved placeholders, merge markers, stale TODOs, or fake links.\\n- Tables, lists, math, and fenced code render plausibly.\\n- Claims with external facts have sources or are clearly marked as assumptions.\\n\\nLaTeX:\\n- The file compiles, or the reason it was not compiled is reported.\\n- No unresolved citations/references in the build log when a PDF is produced.\\n- No obvious placeholder text, broken environments, or empty `\\\\ref{}` / `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n- Generated PDFs correspond to the current `.tex`, not an older build artifact.\\n\\nPDF:\\n- File opens, has at least one page, and is not zero-byte/corrupt.\\n- Expected text is visible/extractable unless the PDF is intentionally image-only.\\n- No accidental blank pages, cropped content, or stale draft/watermark text.\\n\\n## Integration\\n\\nFor repo hooks, CI, and copy/paste setup, read `references/repo-integration.md`.\\nTemplates live in `assets/`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/reinjection/threads/Q3P2/emmy/market_welfare_certificate.md\",\"kind\":\"update\"}],\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"file_change\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/reinjection/threads/Q3P2/emmy/market_welfare_certificate.md\",\"kind\":\"update\"}],\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",