, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 3, 5, 6, 8)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind finding --text 'Cross-check of the table bound and data path: Q \u00a7Experimental Conditions says ten runs per model and states that collected data were released; the assigned inputs contain only Q.tex/P.tex, so exact per-run count/selection decomposition remains unverified here. Q Table 1 count means are exact tenths if they average ten integer counts. Efficiencies are printed to two decimals: allowing each five-row GPT Large efficiency to be rounded by \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\pm0.005\\\\\\\\) changes the aggregate total loss denominator from \\\\\\\\(17.625\\\\\\\\) to at most \\\\\\\\(17.8125\\\\\\\\). Thus the rigorous displayed-data share is at least \\\\\\\\(8/17.8125\\\\\\\\approx44.9\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\), consistent with the earlier approximately \\\\\\\\(45\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\) claim. In round 4 the conservative share is \\\\\\\\(2.7/[7.5(1-0.355)]\\\\\\\\approx55.8\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\). The authors repository README, https://github.com/jswistak/competitive-market-simulation, says the supplementary data package places run files under results and says the reported LLM configurations use unseeded mechanism randomness, although Q \u00a7Experimental Conditions describes ten different random seeds. This discrepancy matters if a follow-up proposes common-seed paired comparisons; it does not by itself invalidate exchangeability of independent runs.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind finding --text 'Cross-check of the table bound and data path: Q \u00a7Experimental Conditions says ten runs per model and states that collected data were released; the assigned inputs contain only Q.tex/P.tex, so exact per-run count/selection decomposition remains unverified here. Q Table 1 count means are exact tenths if they average ten integer counts. Efficiencies are printed to two decimals: allowing each five-row GPT Large efficiency to be rounded by \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\pm0.005\\\\\\\\) changes the aggregate total loss denominator from \\\\\\\\(17.625\\\\\\\\) to at most \\\\\\\\(17.8125\\\\\\\\). Thus the rigorous displayed-data share is at least \\\\\\\\(8/17.8125\\\\\\\\approx44.9\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\), consistent with the earlier approximately \\\\\\\\(45\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\) claim. In round 4 the conservative share is \\\\\\\\(2.7/[7.5(1-0.355)]\\\\\\\\approx55.8\\\\\\\\%\\\\\\\\). The authors repository README, https://github.com/jswistak/competitive-market-simulation, says the supplementary data package places run files under results and says the reported LLM configurations use unseeded mechanism randomness, although Q \u00a7Experimental Conditions describes ten different random seeds. This discrepancy matters if a follow-up proposes common-seed paired comparisons; it does not by itself invalidate exchangeability of independent runs.'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"24\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -c inputs/Q.json inputs/P.json'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'wc -c inputs/Q.json inputs/P.json'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    1588 inputs/Q.json\\n    1279 inputs/P.json\\n    2867 total\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat inputs/Q.json inputs/P.json'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat inputs/Q.json inputs/P.json'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"{\\n \\\"id\\\": \\\"2609.02580\\\",\\n \\\"title\\\": \\\"Competitive Market Behavior of LLMs\\\",\\n \\\"abstract\\\": \\\"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as economic agents, yet there is little evidence whether LLM agents are suited for participating in market mechanisms designed for humans, and whether these mechanisms deliver desired outcomes when faced with LLM agents. We address this question by replicating seminal economic experiments, replacing human subjects with LLM agents. We place agents in a double auction environment, which is a widely-used market mechanism. We check whether such a market is able to deliver an efficient allocation of resources, thereby testing a novel dimension of alignment of LLM agents -- their compatibility with a fundamental market mechanism. We find that markets populated by LLM agents exhibit slower or no convergence towards market equilibrium, thus providing less efficient allocations than markets populated by humans. We then analyze agents' individual trading decisions and find substantial heterogeneity both across model families and market roles. We also run a lexical analysis of Chain-of-Thought (CoT) traces generated by the agents. We find that the decision to execute a trade rather than continue incrementally adjusting prices is associated with a shift from strategic considerations toward urgency. We publicly release our testing framework, which can be used for future evaluations.\\\",\\n \\\"authors\\\": [\\n  \\\"Pawel Struski\\\",\\n  \\\"Jakub Swistak\\\",\\n  \\\"Inez Okulska\\\",\\n  \\\"Przemyslaw Biecek\\\"\\n ],\\n \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-09-02\\\",\\n \\\"text\\\": \\\"sources/2609.02580.tex\\\"\\n}{\\n \\\"id\\\": \\\"pathfinder-local-S2\\\",\\n \\\"title\\\": \\\"Calibrated Selective Constraint Propagation for a Concept-Guided Wall Gate\\\",\\n \\\"abstract\\\": \\\"\\\\nConcept-guided exploration uses an estimated room boundary to restrict later door processing, but a hard geometric threshold can turn a small wall error into a different downstream decision. We isolate the first wall-distance gate of the concept-guided system of Zapata et al. and give it a finite-sample selective certificate. A split-conformal order statistic calibrates a room-level Hausdorff radius from exchangeable, ground-truthed episodes. On the resulting wall-coverage event, every accepted or rejected scan-point decision outside an uncertainty band agrees simultaneously with the decision based on reference walls. This is a marginal guarantee for one interface, not conditional room safety or a certificate for doors, topology, fallback accuracy, or robot motion. The available source papers contain no episode-level wall pairs, so the contribution is a theorem and validation protocol rather than an empirically calibrated system.\\\\n% Ledger: 23, 25, 26, 30, 31, 34, 35.\\\\n\\\",\\n \\\"authors\\\": [\\n  \\\"Pathfinder research campaign\\\"\\n ],\\n \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-09-25\\\",\\n \\\"text\\\": \\\"sources/S2.tex\\\",\\n \\\"source_kind\\\": \\\"local manuscript\\\",\\n \\\"arxiv_id\\\": null\\n}\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada add --kind correction --text 'Precision on #24: inputs/ also contains Q.json and P.json metadata; those files contain abstracts and bibliographic fields, no transaction logs or per-run outcomes. The substantive point is that no run records are present in the assigned input directory. The README link in #24